var e=JSON.parse('[[1801,"The result depends on the method\'s implementation. If the method does not handle null explicitly, that can lead to a NullPointerException (or an equivalent error) when it tries to perform an operation on the value passed in. Some methods may be designed to handle null correctly, returning, for example, a default value or null. You should always check the method\'s documentation or its source code to understand the expected behaviour.","Passing `null` into a method is a widespread scenario that can have different consequences depending on the programming language and the method\'s particular implementation. Understanding this behaviour is critically important for preventing runtime errors and writing reliable code.\\n\\nThe possible behaviours\\n\\n- Throwing an exception (NullPointerException, TypeError): This is the most frequent outcome if the method does not expect `null` and tries to perform an operation on the argument passed in (for example, call its method or access a property).\\n\\n- Correct handling: Some methods are designed to work with `null`. For example, the `Objects.equals()` method in Java compares two objects correctly even if one or both are `null`.\\n\\n- Returning a default value or `null`: A method may interpret `null` as a signal to use a default value, or simply return `null` back.\\n\\n- Undefined behaviour: In languages without strict type checking (for example, in C), passing NULL (a null pointer) can make the program terminate abnormally (a segmentation fault) if the pointer is dereferenced.\\n\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\nWhen designing methods it is important to decide whether a method should accept `null`. If not, it is worth adding an explicit check at the start and throwing a comprehensible exception (for example, `IllegalArgumentException`). If so, that behaviour has to be documented clearly. In modern languages with a type system supporting null safety (for example, Kotlin, or TypeScript in strict mode), the type system itself helps prevent `null` being passed accidentally.\\n\\nConclusion: Passing `null` into a method requires attention both from the method\'s developer (the behaviour has to be defined and documented explicitly) and from its caller (the method\'s contract has to be known). It is always preferable to design methods so that they either forbid `null` explicitly or handle it explicitly and safely, avoiding implicit failures.","public class Example {\\n    // A method that does NOT handle null\\n    public static int getLength(String str) {\\n        return str.length(); // Throws a NullPointerException if str == null\\n    }\\n\\n    // A method that does handle null\\n    public static String safeToUpperCase(String str) {\\n        if (str == null) {\\n            return null; // Explicit handling: we return null\\n        }\\n        return str.toUpperCase();\\n    }\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        // System.out.println(getLength(null)); // NullPointerException!\\n        System.out.println(safeToUpperCase(\\"hello\\")); // HELLO\\n        System.out.println(safeToUpperCase(null));   // null\\n    }\\n}"],[1802,"An endpoint in an HTTP API is a specific URL to which a client can send a request in order to interact with the server. It defines the path to a particular resource or action, for example getting a list of users or creating a new order. Every endpoint is associated with a particular HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE), which states the type of operation. It is a fundamental concept of RESTful architecture that lets you structure an API.","In the context of an HTTP API, an endpoint is an entry point defined by a unique URL through which a client application interacts with the server. It represents a specific resource (for example, a user, a product) or an action that can be performed on that resource.\\nStructure and purpose\\nAn endpoint usually consists of the API\'s base URL and the path to the resource. For example, in the URL `https://api.example.com/users/123` the part `/users/123` is the endpoint for the resource \\"the user with ID 123\\". The key idea is that the same URL can handle different operations depending on the HTTP method:\\n\\n- GET /users — get the list of users.\\n- POST /users — create a new user.\\n- GET /users/{id} — get a particular user\'s data.\\n- PUT /users/{id} — update a user\'s data.\\n- DELETE /users/{id} — delete a user.\\nA code example (Node.js with Express)\\nHere is how simple endpoints can be defined in the Express framework:\\n\\nIn this example `/api/users` and `/api/users/:id` are different endpoints, each handling particular HTTP methods.\\nWhere and how they are used\\nEndpoints are the foundation of any RESTful API, mobile back end or microservice architecture. They let you:\\n\\n- Separate responsibilities clearly between different parts of an application.\\n- Create comprehensible, predictable interfaces for front-end developers or third-party integrations.\\n- Manage access and authentication at the level of individual routes.\\nWell-designed endpoints follow the REST principles, using nouns for resources and HTTP verbs for actions, which makes an API intuitive.\\nConclusion: An endpoint is the basic building block of an HTTP API, defining how clients gain access to the server\'s data and operations. Designing them properly is critically important for creating scalable, maintainable, convenient web services.","const express = require(\'express\');\\nconst app = express();\\napp.use(express.json());\\n\\n// The endpoint for getting all the users (GET)\\napp.get(\'/api/users\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.json([{ id: 1, name: \'Alice\' }]);\\n});\\n\\n// The endpoint for creating a user (POST)\\napp.post(\'/api/users\', (req, res) => {\\n  const newUser = req.body; // The data from the request body\\n  // The logic for saving the user...\\n  res.status(201).json(newUser);\\n});\\n\\n// The endpoint for getting a user by ID (GET with a parameter)\\napp.get(\'/api/users/:id\', (req, res) => {\\n  const userId = req.params.id;\\n  // The logic for finding the user...\\n  res.json({ id: userId, name: \'Bob\' });\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log(\'The API is running on port 3000\'));"],[1803,"GraphQL is a query language for APIs that lets a client request exactly the data it needs and only that data. It solves the problems of REST APIs such as over-fetching and under-fetching. Using it may not be worthwhile, however, for very simple APIs where REST copes perfectly well, or in systems where the performance of complex nested queries is critical. GraphQL also adds complexity on the server side and requires careful design of the schema.","GraphQL is a specification and query language for APIs developed by Facebook that gives clients the ability to define precisely the structure and volume of the data they receive from the server. Unlike REST, where endpoints return fixed data structures, GraphQL lets a client send one request and receive exactly the fields it needs, in the nesting required.\\n\\nThe key concepts and uses\\n\\nGraphQL\'s main components are the schema, which describes the data types and the operations (queries and mutations), and the resolvers — functions that fetch the data for every field. GraphQL is widely used in modern web and mobile applications, especially where flexibility of data and the efficiency of network requests matter, for example in complex dashboards or applications with many views of the same data.\\n\\nAn example of a simple query and schema\\n\\nImagine you have an API for a blog. Instead of making separate requests to `/users/1` and `/users/1/posts`, you can send a single GraphQL query:\\n\\nThe server\'s schema might look like this:\\n\\nWhen GraphQL may not be the best choice\\n\\n- Simple CRUD applications: If your application performs basic create, read, update and delete operations with no complex data requirements, REST or even RPC may be simpler to implement and maintain.\\n\\n- Caching at the HTTP level: REST easily uses the standard HTTP caching mechanisms. GraphQL, which usually uses a single POST endpoint, requires more complex caching solutions (for example, at the level of individual fields).\\n\\n- The performance of complex queries: A client can send a very deep or complex query that causes a great many database connections (the N+1 problem). Solving it requires tools such as DataLoader, which adds complexity.\\n\\n- File uploads: Although this is possible through specifications such as GraphQL Multipart Request, traditional file uploads through REST or a direct PUT/POST are often simpler.\\n\\n- Monitoring and security: Because of the queries\' dynamic nature, they are harder to log, monitor and protect from abuse (for example, excessively deep queries) than fixed REST endpoints.\\n\\nConclusion: GraphQL is a powerful tool for building flexible, efficient APIs, especially when clients have varied and changing data requirements. It should be applied deliberately, however, since for simple scenarios it can add unnecessary complexity, and in high-load systems it requires careful optimisation of the queries.","query {\\n  user(id: 1) {\\n    name\\n    email\\n    posts {\\n      title\\n      createdAt\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ntype User {\\n  id: ID!\\n  name: String!\\n  email: String!\\n  posts: [Post!]\\n}\\n\\ntype Post {\\n  id: ID!\\n  title: String!\\n  content: String!\\n  createdAt: String!\\n}\\n\\ntype Query {\\n  user(id: ID!): User\\n}"],[1804,"An array containing an empty string, `[\\"\\"]`, is a \\"truthy\\" value in JavaScript, since any non-empty array is coerced to `true`. When compared with `false` or `true` using the `==` operator, however, complex type coercion kicks in. For example, `[\\"\\"] == false` returns `true`, because the array is converted into the string `\\"\\"` and an empty string is coerced to `false`. This matters for understanding implicit conversions in conditions and comparisons.","In JavaScript the behaviour of values in comparisons or in a boolean context is determined by the type-coercion mechanism. An array containing an empty string, `[\\"\\"]`, is an interesting case, since it is neither a primitive nor a simple boolean value.\\n\\nThe boolean context (truthy/falsy)\\n\\nIn a boolean context (for example, in an `if` condition) JavaScript coerces a value to a boolean. Values coerced to `false` are called \\"falsy\\". They are: `false`, `0`, `\\"\\"`, `null`, `undefined`, `NaN`. Every other value, including non-empty arrays, objects, functions and strings (even empty ones, if they are inside an array), is \\"truthy\\" and coerced to `true`. So `if ([\\"\\"]) { ... }` runs, because the array `[\\"\\"]` is truthy.\\n\\nComparison with == (loose equality)\\n\\nWhen the loose equality operator `==` is used, JavaScript performs complex type coercion if the operands are of different types. Consider the comparison `[\\"\\"] == false`.\\n\\n- According to the specification, if one of the operands is a boolean (`false`), it is first converted to a number: `false` becomes `0`.\\n\\n- The comparison is now: `[\\"\\"] == 0`.\\n\\n- Since one operand is an object (an array) and the other a primitive (a number), the array is converted to a primitive. For arrays `valueOf()` is called first, which returns the array itself (not a primitive), then `toString()`. `[\\"\\"]` is converted to a string: the empty string inside the array is joined by a comma, but since there is only one element, the result is simply `\\"\\"`.\\n\\n- The comparison is now: `\\"\\" == 0`.\\n\\n- The string `\\"\\"` is converted to a number: an empty string becomes `0`.\\n\\n- The result: `0 == 0` → `true`.\\n\\nSo `[\\"\\"] == false` returns `true`, which often surprises developers.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nWhere does this apply?\\n\\nUnderstanding this behaviour is critical when working with conditional expressions, with form validation (where values may arrive as arrays), and in comparisons, especially in legacy code where `==` is often used. It helps you avoid elusive bugs.\\n\\nConclusion: An array containing an empty string, `[\\"\\"]`, is truthy, but in a loose comparison with `false` it returns `true` because of the chain of type conversions. To avoid surprises it is recommended to use strict equality `===` and explicit checks, for example `if (array.length > 0)` for arrays.","// The boolean context\\nif ([\\"\\"]) {\\n    console.log(\\"The array is truthy - this block runs\\");\\n}\\n\\n// Loose equality\\nconsole.log([\\"\\"] == false); // true\\nconsole.log([\\"\\"] == true);  // false (since true becomes 1, \\"\\" becomes 0, and 0 != 1)\\n\\n// Strict equality (no type coercion)\\nconsole.log([\\"\\"] === false); // false\\n\\n// Converting the array to a primitive\\nconsole.log([\\"\\"].toString()); // \\"\\"\\nconsole.log(Number([\\"\\"]));    // 0\\n\\n// Comparison with an empty string\\nconsole.log([\\"\\"] == \\"\\"); // true (the array becomes \\"\\", and \\"\\" == \\"\\")\\nconsole.log([\\"\\"] === \\"\\"); // false (different types)"],[1805,"In most modern programming languages identical string literals are usually not created as different objects in memory, thanks to the string interning mechanism. For example, in Java and Python string literals are interned automatically, which means identical strings refer to the same object in the string pool. That saves memory and speeds up string comparison with the `==` operator in some languages, but for reliable comparison it is still recommended to use the `equals()` method or its equivalents.","String literals are strings given directly in the source code in quotes. The question of whether identical literals are created as different objects touches on a key optimisation — string interning.\\nWhat is string interning?\\nInterning is the process of holding only one copy of each unique string value in a special area of memory called the string pool. When the same string literal is encountered in the code, instead of creating a new object the system returns a reference to the string that already exists in the pool.\\nHow does it work in different languages?\\n\\n- Java: Every string literal is interned automatically. That happens while the class is being loaded.\\n- Python: Strings are also interned, but the mechanism is more complex and can depend on the implementation (CPython).\\n- C#: Literals are interned, and for strings created at runtime you can use the `String.Intern()` method.\\nA code example in Java\\n\\nIn this example `s1` and `s2` refer to the same object, while `s3` refers to a new one, because it was created through the constructor.\\nPractical use and conclusion\\nInterning saves memory, especially when a program has many repeated strings. It also speeds up comparing strings with `==`, since the equality of references is checked rather than a character-by-character comparison. Relying on that to compare strings created dynamically is dangerous, however — always use methods that compare by value, such as `equals()` in Java or `==` in Python (which for strings compares the values, thanks to interning and optimisations).\\nSummary: Identical string literals are usually not created as different objects, thanks to interning, which is an important optimisation for working with strings in most languages.","String s1 = \\"hello\\";\\nString s2 = \\"hello\\";\\nString s3 = new String(\\"hello\\");\\n\\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s2); // true: one object in the pool\\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s3); // false: s3 is a new object on the heap\\nSystem.out.println(s1.equals(s3)); // true: the contents are the same"],[1806,"A structure can be made abstract if it is declared with the `abstract` modifier and cannot be instantiated directly. Abstract structures serve as templates for other classes, defining a common interface and a partial implementation. They are used to provide polymorphism and to enforce contracts in an inheritance hierarchy. For example, in Java or C# an abstract class can contain both abstract methods (with no implementation) and concrete ones.","In object-oriented programming an abstract structure is a class or interface that cannot be instantiated directly. Its main purpose is to serve as a base template for other classes, defining a common contract (the methods) and possibly providing part of the implementation.\\nWhy are abstract structures needed?\\nThey help you:\\n\\n- Define a common interface: guarantee that every derived class implements particular methods.\\n- Use polymorphism: work with objects of different types through a single base type.\\n- Avoid duplicated code: move the shared logic into the abstract class.\\n- Provide architectural clarity: separate the abstraction and the concrete implementation explicitly.\\nAn example in Java\\nConsider an abstract `Shape` class that defines the common properties for every shape.\\n\\nWhere are they used?\\nAbstract structures are widely used in:\\n\\n- Frameworks (for example, Spring, .NET), to define components\' base classes.\\n- Libraries, where an interface has to be specified for plugins or extensions.\\n- Design patterns (Factory Method, Template Method, Strategy).\\nConclusion: abstract structures are worth applying when you need to specify a common contract for a family of classes, provide polymorphic behaviour and avoid duplicated code. They are a key tool for building a flexible, extensible architecture.","// An abstract class - you cannot create an object with new Shape()\\npublic abstract class Shape {\\n    protected String color;\\n    \\n    public Shape(String color) {\\n        this.color = color;\\n    }\\n    \\n    // An abstract method - it must be implemented in the subclasses\\n    public abstract double area();\\n    \\n    // A concrete method - a shared implementation for every shape\\n    public String getColor() {\\n        return color;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// A concrete subclass\\npublic class Circle extends Shape {\\n    private double radius;\\n    \\n    public Circle(String color, double radius) {\\n        super(color);\\n        this.radius = radius;\\n    }\\n    \\n    @Override\\n    public double area() {\\n        return Math.PI * radius * radius;\\n    }\\n}"],[1807,"Frequent string concatenation with the \'+\' operator in languages with immutable strings (Java, C#, Python) creates many intermediate objects, which reduces performance. To optimise it, use special classes such as StringBuilder in Java/.NET, or the join() method. These classes hold the characters in a mutable buffer, avoiding unnecessary copying. In Python it is efficient to use the str.join() method, or f-strings for the final formatting.","In programming languages where strings are immutable, every concatenation operation creates a new string object by copying the contents of the previous ones. That leads to quadratic complexity, O(n²), in time and memory when assembling a string from many parts in a loop.\\nWhy is it a problem?\\nImagine you are about to glue 1000 strings together. Using `result += part`, a new string is created on every iteration and all the previous content is copied again. That is extremely inefficient.\\nHow do you optimise it?\\nThe solution is to use data structures intended for building strings in stages:\\n\\n- Java, C#: The `StringBuilder` (Java, .NET) and `StringBuffer` (thread-safe) classes. They hold the characters in a mutable array that grows dynamically.\\n- Python: The `str.join()` method on a list of strings, or using a list followed by `join`. For formatting, use f-strings or `str.format()`.\\n- JavaScript: An array with the `join()` method, or template literals.\\n- Go: The `strings.Builder` type.\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nConclusion: Use specialised string-builder classes (`StringBuilder`, `strings.Builder`) or the `join` method for frequent concatenation in loops. That is critically important for performance when working with large volumes of text data, for example when generating HTML, JSON or logs.","// Inefficient\\nString result = \\"\\";\\nfor (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n    result += \\"some data \\"; // Creates a new String object at every step\\n}\\n\\n// Optimal\\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\nfor (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n    sb.append(\\"some data \\");\\n}\\nString result = sb.toString();\\n\\n# Inefficient\\nresult = \\"\\"\\nfor part in large_list:\\n    result += part\\n\\n# Optimal\\nresult = \\"\\".join(large_list)\\n\\n# Or through a list, if the strings are collected in a loop\\nparts = []\\nfor part in some_generator:\\n    parts.append(part)\\nresult = \\"\\".join(parts)"],[1808,"Extending behaviour means adding new functionality to the child class without touching the parent\'s existing methods. Changing behaviour is overriding the parent class\'s methods in the child so that they work differently. Extending is safe and conforms to the Liskov substitution principle. Changing behaviour requires care, so as not to break the base class\'s contract and the client code\'s expectations.","Inheritance is an object-oriented programming mechanism that lets one class (the child) receive the fields and methods of another class (the parent). It is important to distinguish two main purposes for using inheritance: extending and changing the parent class\'s behaviour.\\n\\nExtending behaviour\\n\\nExtending means the child class adds new functionality without touching what it inherited. This is the safest and most preferable way of using inheritance. The child class remains fully compatible with the parent and can be used anywhere the parent type is expected (the Liskov substitution principle).\\n\\nChanging behaviour\\n\\nChanging behaviour happens when the child class overrides a parent\'s method, altering its logic. This is a powerful tool for implementing polymorphism, but it requires care so as not to break the base class\'s contract (for example, by changing the return type or weakening the preconditions).\\n\\nPractical use and recommendations\\n\\n- Extending is often used to create more specialised classes that add unique properties or methods (for example, `SportsCar extends Car`).\\n\\n- Changing (overriding) is critically important for implementing polymorphic behaviour in patterns such as Strategy, or for providing a specific implementation of an abstract method.\\n\\n- When changing behaviour you should observe the Liskov substitution principle: client code working with the base class must work correctly with objects of the child class too, without knowing about the substitution.\\n\\nConclusion: Use extension when you need to add new functionality while preserving everything that exists. Use a change of behaviour (overriding) deliberately, when inherited logic has to be modified, and make sure that does not break the base class\'s contract and the expectations of its public interface.","class Vehicle {\\n    startEngine() {\\n        console.log(\'Engine started\');\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass Car extends Vehicle {\\n    // Extension: a new method has been added\\n    turnOnAirConditioning() {\\n        console.log(\'AC is on\');\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nconst myCar = new Car();\\nmyCar.startEngine(); // The inherited method works as it is\\nmyCar.turnOnAirConditioning(); // The new functionality\\n\\nclass Bird {\\n    fly() {\\n        console.log(\'Flying high\');\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass Penguin extends Bird {\\n    // A change of behaviour: overriding the method\\n    fly() {\\n        console.log(\'Sorry, I cannot fly\');\\n        // Perhaps there will be other logic here, for example swimming\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nconst bird = new Bird();\\nbird.fly(); // Output: Flying high\\n\\nconst penguin = new Penguin();\\npenguin.fly(); // Output: Sorry, I cannot fly"],[1809,"String interning is a mechanism whereby identical string literals, or strings created explicitly, are held in memory as a single instance. That saves memory and speeds up string comparison operations (for example, with `==` in Java), since references are compared rather than characters. The mechanism is usually applied to string literals in the source code and sometimes to strings created dynamically (for example, through `String.intern()` in Java). In Python, short strings and strings that are identifiers are interned.","String interning is an optimisation technique used in many programming languages (such as Java, C#, Python) that guarantees strings with the same content will be represented by the same object in memory. That is achieved by maintaining a special data structure — the string pool — where unique string values are held.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nWhen a string literal (for example, `\\"hello\\"`) is encountered in the code, the compiler or virtual machine checks whether such a string already exists in the pool. If it does, a reference to the existing object is returned; if not, the string is added to the pool and a new object is created. That makes strings immutable, since changing one string could affect every reference to it.\\n\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nIn Python interning is applied automatically to short strings (usually consisting of letters, digits and underscores) and to all string literals that are identifiers (for example, variable names). You can check with the `is` operator.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Memory optimisation: it prevents the duplication of identical strings, which is critical in applications working with a large amount of text data.\\n\\n- Faster comparison: comparing references (`==` in Java for interned strings) is faster than a character-by-character comparison.\\n\\n- Caching: it is often used inside languages to hold method names, class fields and constants.\\n\\nConclusion: String interning is worth applying when an application has many repeated string values (for example, when parsing CSV or working with tokens) and saving memory matters. An explicit call to `intern()` (in Java) can increase the running time if there are too many strings, however, since the pool requires synchronisation. Use it deliberately, relying mainly on the automatic interning of literals.","String s1 = \\"hello\\";\\nString s2 = \\"hello\\";\\nString s3 = new String(\\"hello\\");\\nString s4 = s3.intern();\\n\\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s2); // true: both literals point at the same object in the pool\\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s3); // false: s3 is a new object on the heap\\nSystem.out.println(s1 == s4); // true: intern() put the string in the pool and returned the reference\\n\\na = \\"hello\\"\\nb = \\"hello\\"\\nc = \\"hel\\" + \\"lo\\"\\nprint(a is b)  # True: they are interned\\nprint(a is c)  # True: the concatenation of literals is optimised\\n\\nx = \\"hello!\\"\\ny = \\"hello!\\"\\nprint(x is y)  # May be False in some cases, since \\"!\\" can prevent interning"],[1810,"Strings are immutable because that provides security, performance and simplicity of design. For example, immutability prevents the accidental modification of string literals that may be used in several places in a program. It also lets a string\'s hash code be cached, which speeds up hash tables. In addition, immutable objects are thread-safe by their very nature, which simplifies multithreaded programming.","The immutability of strings is a fundamental design decision in many programming languages, such as Java, C#, Python and JavaScript. It means that once a string object has been created its contents cannot be changed. Any operation that seemingly changes a string (for example, concatenation or replacing characters) in fact creates a new string object.\\n\\nThe main reasons for immutability\\n\\n- Security: Strings are often used to hold confidential data (passwords, URLs, the parameters of system calls). If strings were mutable, malicious code could change their contents after they were passed along, which would lead to vulnerabilities.\\n\\n- Performance and optimisation: Compilers and runtimes use a technique called the string pool (string interning). When a string literal is created, it is placed in the pool. If the same literal is created later, a reference to the existing object is returned, which saves memory. That optimisation is possible only thanks to the guarantee of immutability.\\n\\n- Caching the hash code: Since a string\'s contents never change, its hash code can be computed once at creation and cached. That is critically important for the efficient operation of hash-based data structures such as HashMap or HashSet.\\n\\n- Thread safety: Immutable objects can be passed between threads safely with no need for synchronisation. That eliminates a whole class of race-condition errors.\\n\\n- Simplicity of design: Immutability makes strings\' behaviour predictable. When you pass a string into a method, you can be sure it will not be changed somewhere deep in the call.\\n\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nThis example shows that even after the `toUpperCase()` method is called, the original string `str1` has not changed. The method returns a completely new object.\\n\\nConclusion: The immutability of strings is a trade-off that sacrifices the potential efficiency of individual operations (for example, concatenation in a loop) for global benefits: security, stability, memory optimisation and simpler multithreaded code. It is one of the key reasons why strings are implemented this way in most modern languages.","public class StringExample {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        String str1 = \\"Hello\\";\\n        // str1 is placed in the string pool\\n        String str2 = \\"Hello\\";\\n        // str2 receives a reference to the same object from the pool\\n        System.out.println(str1 == str2); // true (the same references)\\n\\n        // A concatenation operation creates a new object\\n        String str3 = str1 + \\" World\\";\\n        System.out.println(str3); // \\"Hello World\\"\\n        // The original string str1 is still \\"Hello\\"\\n\\n        // An attempt to \\"change\\" the string through a method\\n        String upper = str1.toUpperCase();\\n        System.out.println(str1); // Still \\"Hello\\"\\n        System.out.println(upper); // A new object, \\"HELLO\\"\\n    }\\n}"],[1811,"Asynchrony is a way of organising code where tasks can run without blocking the main thread while waiting for long operations (for example, I/O) to finish. Parallelism is the simultaneous execution of several tasks on different processor cores. Asynchrony is often used for responsiveness in single-threaded environments, while parallelism is used to speed up computation by using several cores.","Asynchrony and parallelism are two different models of managing the execution of tasks in programming that are often confused. Understanding their differences is critical for choosing the right approach to optimising performance.\\n\\nAsynchronous execution\\n\\nAsynchrony is a programming style where the execution of a task can be suspended to let another task run, without waiting for the first to finish. The key idea is non-blocking waiting. That is especially useful for I/O operations, such as reading files, network requests or waiting for user input, where the processor sits idle waiting for data.\\n\\nIn JavaScript asynchrony is implemented through the Event Loop mechanism. A long task (for example, a fetch request) is sent into the background while the main thread carries on handling other events. When the background task finishes, its result is queued for processing.\\n\\nParallelism\\n\\nParallelism is the physical simultaneous execution of several tasks on different processor cores or threads. The goal is to speed up computationally heavy (CPU-bound) tasks by dividing the work between several cores.\\n\\nParallelism requires support at the level of the operating system and the hardware (a multi-core CPU). The classic example is the use of multithreading in languages such as Java, C# or Python (the multiprocessing module).\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- The goal: Asynchrony improves responsiveness and makes efficient use of the idle time spent waiting for I/O. Parallelism increases throughput for CPU-bound tasks.\\n\\n- Resources: Asynchrony often works on a single thread (for example, Node.js). Parallelism requires several threads or processes.\\n\\n- Blocking: Asynchronous code does not block the thread while waiting. Parallel threads can block if they need a shared resource.\\n\\n- Area of application: Asynchrony is ideal for web servers and for handling many network connections. Parallelism is for scientific computing, image processing and complex simulations.\\n\\nConclusion: Use asynchrony to optimise operations involving waiting (I/O-bound tasks, for example web requests or database work). Use parallelism to speed up computations that load the processor (CPU-bound tasks, for example sorting large arrays or machine learning). These approaches are often combined to achieve maximum performance.","// An example of asynchronous code in JavaScript (async/await)\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  console.log(\'Starting the request...\');\\n  // fetch does not block the thread; execution carries on\\n  const response = await fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\');\\n  const data = await response.json(); // Waiting here does not block other tasks\\n  console.log(\'The data has arrived:\', data);\\n  return data;\\n}\\nconsole.log(\'Before the function call\');\\nfetchData();\\nconsole.log(\'After the function call - this runs immediately, without waiting for the fetch\');\\n\\n# An example of parallel computation in Python using multiprocessing\\nimport multiprocessing\\nimport time\\n\\ndef compute_square(number):\\n    # Simulating a heavy CPU task\\n    time.sleep(0.5)\\n    return number * number\\n\\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\\n    numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\\n    # Create a pool of processes for parallel execution\\n    with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=2) as pool:\\n        results = pool.map(compute_square, numbers)  # The tasks run in parallel\\n    print(\'The results:\', results)  # [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]"],[1812,"The Factory pattern should be applied when the logic for creating an object becomes complex, for example when it depends on conditions or configuration. It is useful when you need to create families of related objects without tying the code to concrete classes. That makes it easier to add new types of object and makes the code more flexible and testable.","The Factory pattern is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for creating objects in a superclass, letting subclasses change the type of object created. The main goal is to encapsulate the creation process, separating the code that uses an object from the code that creates it.\\n\\nWhen should Factory be applied?\\n\\n- When creating an object requires complex logic (for example, choosing a subclass based on the configuration, the input or the system\'s state).\\n\\n- When you need to create families of related or dependent objects without tying yourself to their concrete classes.\\n\\n- When you want to make it easy to extend the system with new types of object.\\n\\n- When the implementation details of creating an object have to be hidden, to simplify the client code and improve testability (the factory can be substituted in tests).\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a logging system that can write messages to the console, to a file or send them over the network. Instead of checking the configuration and creating a concrete instance in every place a logger is needed, we use a factory.\\n\\nNow, if a new type of logger has to be added (for example, for a database), it is enough to change the factory without touching all the rest of the code that uses the `Logger` interface. That conforms to the open/closed principle.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nThe Factory pattern is worth applying when the process of creating an object is not trivial and you want to achieve loose coupling, flexibility and ease of maintenance. It is especially useful in large applications where the types of object may change or be extended.","// The logger interface\\ninterface Logger {\\n    log(message: string): void;\\n}\\n\\n// The concrete implementations\\nclass ConsoleLogger implements Logger {\\n    log(message: string) { console.log(message); }\\n}\\nclass FileLogger implements Logger {\\n    log(message: string) { /* writing to a file */ }\\n}\\n\\n// The factory\\nclass LoggerFactory {\\n    static createLogger(type: string): Logger {\\n        switch(type) {\\n            case \'console\': return new ConsoleLogger();\\n            case \'file\': return new FileLogger();\\n            default: throw new Error(\'Unknown logger type\');\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst logger = LoggerFactory.createLogger(\'console\');\\nlogger.log(\'Application started\');"],[1813,"The Strategy pattern should be applied when you have several related algorithms or variants of behaviour and need to choose between them while the program runs. It is useful when a class contains many conditional statements for choosing its behaviour. This pattern encapsulates each algorithm in a separate class, making them interchangeable. That makes it easy to add new algorithms without changing the existing client code. Strategy helps you observe the SOLID principles, such as open/closed and single responsibility.","The Strategy pattern is a behavioural design pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each of them and makes them interchangeable. That lets you choose the algorithm you need while the program is running rather than at compile time.\\n\\nThe main idea and its use\\n\\nThe main goal is to separate an algorithm from the object that uses it. That is especially useful in systems where an object\'s behaviour has to change dynamically depending on the context or on user input. For example, a navigation application may use different route-building algorithms (on foot, by car, by public transport) that can be switched at the user\'s wish.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider a simple example of a payment system supporting different methods (CreditCard, PayPal, Crypto). Instead of piling conditional statements into the order class, we extract strategies.\\n\\nThe key advantages\\n\\n- Getting rid of conditional statements: It removes long switch/case or if/else blocks.\\n\\n- Flexibility and extensibility: New algorithms are added easily, without modifying the context\'s existing code.\\n\\n- Observing SOLID: Every strategy has one responsibility, while the context is open for extension.\\n\\n- Testability: The strategies can be tested in isolation.\\n\\nConclusion: The Strategy pattern is worth applying when a system has several variations of one algorithm or behaviour that may change depending on external conditions, and you want to keep the code clean, flexible and easy to maintain. It is ideally suited to scenarios where rules or logic are often added or changed.","// The strategy interface\\ninterface PaymentStrategy {\\n    pay(amount: number): void;\\n}\\n\\n// The concrete strategies\\nclass CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\\n    pay(amount: number) {\\n        console.log(`Paying ${amount} using Credit Card.`);\\n        // The card-processing logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass PayPalPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\\n    pay(amount: number) {\\n        console.log(`Paying ${amount} using PayPal.`);\\n        // The PayPal API logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The context, which uses a strategy\\nclass ShoppingCart {\\n    private strategy: PaymentStrategy;\\n\\n    constructor(strategy: PaymentStrategy) {\\n        this.strategy = strategy;\\n    }\\n\\n    setPaymentStrategy(strategy: PaymentStrategy) {\\n        this.strategy = strategy; // Changing the strategy dynamically\\n    }\\n\\n    checkout(amount: number) {\\n        this.strategy.pay(amount);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst cart = new ShoppingCart(new CreditCardPayment());\\ncart.checkout(100); // Paying 100 using Credit Card.\\n\\ncart.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment());\\ncart.checkout(200); // Paying 200 using PayPal."],[1814,"Mutable objects can be modified after creation, changing their internal state. Immutable objects cannot be changed after creation — any operation that looks like a change creates a new object. For example, in Python a list is mutable while a tuple is immutable. Using immutable objects makes code simpler to understand and debug, since their state is fixed.","The concept of mutability is fundamental in programming and determines whether an object\'s internal state can be changed after it has been created. Understanding this distinction is critically important for writing reliable, predictable code, especially in multithreaded environments or when using functional paradigms.\\n\\nMutable objects\\n\\nMutable objects let their data be modified. That means a variable referring to the object carries on pointing at the same region of memory even when the object\'s internal content has changed. Typical examples: lists in Python, objects in JavaScript (unless special techniques are used), arrays in most languages.\\n\\nImmutable objects\\n\\nImmutable objects, by contrast, do not let their state be changed after creation. Any operation that looks like a change (for example, string concatenation) in fact creates a completely new object in memory. Examples: numbers, strings (str), tuples in Python; the primitive values (number, string) in JavaScript.\\n\\nPractical use and consequences\\n\\nThe choice between a mutable and an immutable approach affects an application\'s architecture:\\n\\n- Predictability: Immutable objects rule out unexpected side effects when several parts of the code refer to the same data.\\n\\n- Thread safety: Immutable objects are safe to use in multithreaded programs by their very nature, since their state cannot be changed concurrently.\\n\\n- Caching and optimisation: Compilers and interpreters can perform optimisations (for example, string interning), knowing that immutable objects will never change.\\n\\n- Debugging: With immutable objects it is easier to track the system\'s state at a particular moment in time.\\n\\nConclusion: Use immutable objects and approaches (for example, creating new objects instead of mutating) in situations where predictability, thread safety or the tracking of state changes (as in Redux) matter. Mutable objects are appropriate when high performance is required for frequent modifications of large data structures and you control their scope completely.","# An example of a mutable object (a Python list)\\nlist_a = [1, 2, 3]\\nlist_b = list_a          # list_b refers to the same object\\nlist_a.append(4)         # Change the original list\\nprint(list_b)            # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4] - list_b changed too!\\n\\n# An example of an immutable object (a Python string)\\nstr_a = \\"Hello\\"\\nstr_b = str_a            # str_b refers to the same object\\nstr_a += \\" World!\\"      # A NEW string is created; str_a now points at it\\nprint(str_a)            # Output: \\"Hello World!\\"\\nprint(str_b)            # Output: \\"Hello\\" - str_b is unchanged"],[1815,"CPU-bound tasks are limited by the processor\'s performance; they require intensive computation. IO-bound tasks are limited by input/output operations, such as reading files or network requests. For CPU-bound tasks, parallelising across several cores is effective. For IO-bound tasks, asynchrony suits better, so as not to block the thread while waiting. Understanding the difference helps you choose the right approach to optimisation.","In programming, tasks are often classified by which resource is the main bottleneck for their execution. That knowledge is critically important for designing an application\'s architecture, choosing a concurrency model and allocating resources.\\n\\nCPU-bound tasks\\n\\nCPU-bound tasks require intensive computation. They load the central processor, and their speed of execution depends directly on the CPU\'s clock rate and number of cores. Examples: complex mathematical calculations (for example, factorising numbers, simulations), image processing, machine learning during the model-training stage.\\n\\nTo speed up CPU-bound tasks it is effective to use parallel computation, distributing the work between several processor cores or machines.\\n\\nIO-bound tasks\\n\\nIO-bound tasks spend most of their time waiting for input/output operations to finish. Those operations may be: reading/writing files on disk, database queries, network calls (HTTP, WebSocket), waiting for user input. The processor sits idle meanwhile.\\n\\nTo optimise IO-bound tasks, asynchronous programming and non-blocking operations are used. That lets a single thread handle many tasks while they wait for IO, instead of creating a separate thread for each.\\n\\nPractical use and choosing an approach\\n\\n- A web server: Handling HTTP requests is a typical IO-bound task (the network, the database). Use asynchronous frameworks (Node.js, FastAPI) for high concurrency.\\n\\n- Scientific computing: Analysing large data is CPU-bound. Use multithreading (Python\'s multiprocessing) or distributed systems.\\n\\n- Hybrid tasks: Web scraping: network requests (IO) plus parsing HTML (CPU). A combination of asynchrony for the IO and a thread pool for the CPU part is often used.\\n\\nConclusion: Understanding the type of constraint on a task is the key to effective optimisation. For CPU-bound tasks, focus on parallelism and faster hardware. For IO-bound tasks, focus on asynchrony, caching and optimising the IO operations themselves (for example, using an SSD).","// An example of a CPU-bound task: computing pi\\nfunction calculatePi(iterations) {\\n    let pi = 0;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {\\n        // The Leibniz formula\\n        pi += Math.pow(-1, i) / (2 * i + 1);\\n    }\\n    return pi * 4;\\n}\\n// A large number of iterations will load the CPU.\\n\\n// An example of an IO-bound task: reading several files\\nconst fs = require(\'fs/promises\');\\n\\nasync function readFiles(filePaths) {\\n    const contents = [];\\n    for (const path of filePaths) {\\n        // Waiting for the read from disk to finish is an IO operation.\\n        const data = await fs.readFile(path, \'utf8\');\\n        contents.push(data);\\n    }\\n    return contents;\\n}"],[1816,"Missing keys in data are a frequent problem, especially when working with APIs or user input. The main approaches include checking that a key exists before accessing it, using the optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`) operators in JavaScript/TypeScript, and setting default values. It is also important to validate the data at an early stage, to avoid errors deep in the code. These methods make the code more reliable and predictable.","When receiving data from external sources such as APIs, configuration files or user forms, you can never be completely sure of its structure. A key you expect to find in an object may be missing, which leads to an error like `Cannot read property \'...\' of undefined` and possibly to the application crashing. Handling missing keys is therefore a fundamental skill for building resilient software.\\n\\nThe main handling strategies\\n\\nThere are several widespread approaches, which can be combined:\\n\\n- Checking that a key exists: An explicit check with conditions before accessing nested properties.\\n\\n- Optional chaining (`?.`): A modern operator that lets you access the properties of deeply nested objects safely without raising an error if some intermediate property is `null` or `undefined`.\\n\\n- Nullish coalescing (`??`): An operator that returns its right operand only if the left one is `null` or `undefined`. Ideally suited to supplying default values.\\n\\n- Default parameters: In functions you can set default values for parameters, which is especially useful when destructuring objects.\\n\\n- Validating the data on input: Using validation libraries (for example, Joi, Yup, Zod) or writing your own checks, to make sure the data matches the expected schema before using it in the business logic.\\n\\nCode examples in JavaScript\\n\\nConsider a user object that may arrive from an API:\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nThese techniques are universal and are used wherever data is handled: front-end applications processing a server\'s responses; back-end services parsing incoming HTTP requests; scripts for processing configuration files. They matter especially in a microservice architecture, where services communicate with each other and the data format may change.\\n\\nConclusion: Handling missing keys is a mandatory practice for writing error-resilient code. Use optional chaining and default values for brevity and readability, and for critically important data supplement them with strict validation at the system\'s boundaries.","// The input data: the object may be incomplete\\nconst apiResponse = {\\n    id: 123,\\n    // The \'profile\' property may be missing\\n};\\n\\n// 1. The classic check (verbose but reliable)\\nlet userName1 = \'Guest\';\\nif (apiResponse.profile && apiResponse.profile.name) {\\n    userName1 = apiResponse.profile.name;\\n}\\n\\n// 2. Using optional chaining and nullish coalescing (the modern way)\\nconst userName2 = apiResponse?.profile?.name ?? \'Guest\';\\n\\n// 3. Destructuring with default parameters\\nfunction getUserInfo({ profile = {} } = {}) {\\n    const { name = \'Guest\' } = profile;\\n    return name;\\n}\\nconst userName3 = getUserInfo(apiResponse);\\n\\nconsole.log(userName1, userName2, userName3); // All print \'Guest\'"],[1817,"In data validation, errors can arise relating to an incorrect format, type or range, or to missing mandatory fields. For example, passing a string instead of a number, an email with no \'@\', or a date in the future for a \\"date of birth\\" field. Security errors are also frequent, such as SQL injection or XSS, if the data is not escaped. Proper validation on both the client and the server helps prevent failures and vulnerabilities.","Data validation is the process of checking input information against the expected rules, formats and constraints. Its purpose is to ensure an application works correctly, to protect it from erroneous or malicious data and to preserve the integrity of the information in the system. Errors in validation can lead to failures in the logic, to security vulnerabilities or to corrupted data.\\n\\nThe main categories of validation error\\n\\n- Format errors: The data does not match the required pattern (for example, an email with no \'@\', a phone number with letters).\\n\\n- Type errors: The wrong data type was passed (a string instead of a number, an array instead of an object).\\n\\n- Range/length errors: The value falls outside the permitted bounds (a negative age, a string longer than 255 characters).\\n\\n- Mandatory-field errors: A required field is missing or empty.\\n\\n- Logical consistency errors: The data contradicts itself (an end date earlier than the start date).\\n\\n- Security errors: The data contains dangerous constructs (SQL injection, XSS scripts).\\n\\nExamples and practical use\\n\\nValidation is applied at every level of an application: in forms on the front end, in the API on the back end and when writing to a database. Consider an example of validation on the server using Node.js and the Joi library.\\n\\nIn this example the validation schema describes the rules for every field explicitly. If any of them is breached, a detailed error is returned that can be handled and reported to the user.\\n\\nCommon vulnerabilities caused by insufficient validation\\n\\n- SQL injection: Arises if user input is inserted directly into an SQL query without escaping.\\n\\n- XSS (cross-site scripting): Possible if unescaped HTML or JavaScript is printed onto a page.\\n\\n- Insecure deserialisation: Can lead to arbitrary code execution when serialised objects are processed.\\n\\nConclusion: Data validation is a critically important stage of development. It should always be applied when receiving data from untrusted sources (user input, external APIs). Deep validation on the server side is mandatory for security, while validation on the client improves UX by giving fast feedback.","const Joi = require(\'joi\');\\n\\nconst userSchema = Joi.object({\\n  email: Joi.string().email().required(),\\n  age: Joi.number().integer().min(0).max(120).required(),\\n  password: Joi.string().min(8).pattern(/[A-Z]/).required(),\\n  subscriptionDate: Joi.date().max(\'now\').required()\\n});\\n\\nconst userInput = {\\n  email: \'user@example\', // An error: an incomplete email\\n  age: 150,               // An error: the maximum is exceeded\\n  password: \'weak\',       // An error: too short\\n  subscriptionDate: \'2025-01-01\' // An error: a date in the future\\n};\\n\\nconst { error } = userSchema.validate(userInput);\\nif (error) {\\n  console.log(\'Validation errors:\', error.details.map(d => d.message));\\n  // Prints the list of all the breaches\\n}"],[1818,"WebSocket is a communication protocol on top of TCP that establishes a persistent two-way connection between the client and the server. Unlike HTTP, which works on a request-response model and requires a new connection for every exchange, WebSocket lets data be transferred in real time with minimal overhead. That makes it ideal for chats, online games and financial tickers, where instant data transfer matters.","WebSocket is a protocol providing a full-duplex communication channel over a single TCP connection. It is designed to overcome the limitations of HTTP, which was not originally intended for a constant exchange of messages.\\nHow the connection is established\\nThe connection begins with a handshake over HTTP. The client sends a special HTTP request with the header `Upgrade: websocket`. If the server supports WebSocket, it replies with a confirmation, after which the protocol switches from HTTP to WebSocket. From that moment the connection stays open and both sides can send data independently of each other.\\nThe key differences from HTTP\\n\\n- The communication model: HTTP is strictly request-response; the client always initiates the exchange. WebSocket is an equal, two-way exchange.\\n- The connection: HTTP usually creates a new connection for every request (although Keep-Alive exists). WebSocket uses one persistent connection.\\n- Overhead: HTTP headers can be large for every request. In WebSocket, after the handshake the data is transferred as lightweight frames with minimal overhead.\\n- Who can send: In HTTP the server cannot send data to the client without a request from it. WebSocket lets the server send messages to the client at any moment.\\nA code example in JavaScript\\nBelow is a basic example of using the WebSocket API in the browser.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nWebSocket is ideally suited to applications requiring low latency and a constant exchange of data: online chats, real-time notifications, multiplayer games, trading platforms with live quotes, collaborative editors and monitoring tools.\\nConclusion: Use WebSocket when your application requires a constant two-way exchange of messages with minimal latency and HTTP\'s request-response model becomes inefficient.","// Creating the connection\\nconst socket = new WebSocket(\'wss://echo.websocket.org\');\\n\\n// The handler for the connection opening\\nsocket.onopen = function(event) {\\n  console.log(\'The connection is established\');\\n  socket.send(\'Hello, server!\'); // Sending a message\\n};\\n\\n// The handler for incoming messages\\nsocket.onmessage = function(event) {\\n  console.log(\'Received from the server:\', event.data);\\n};\\n\\n// The error handler\\nsocket.onerror = function(error) {\\n  console.error(\'WebSocket error:\', error);\\n};\\n\\n// The handler for the connection closing\\nsocket.onclose = function(event) {\\n  console.log(\'The connection is closed\');\\n};\\n\\n// Closing the connection after 10 seconds\\nsetTimeout(() => socket.close(), 10000);"],[1819,"A memory cell is the minimal physical unit of data storage in a computer\'s random-access memory (RAM). Every cell has a unique address and can hold one bit of information — 0 or 1. Groups of cells are combined to hold bytes (usually 8 bits) and larger data. Understanding the cell matters for grasping how the processor reads and writes data.","A memory cell is the fundamental building block of computer memory, a physical device (for example, a capacitor in DRAM or a flip-flop in SRAM) capable of holding the minimal portion of information — one bit. Every cell has a unique numeric address by which the processor or the memory controller can access it to read or write a value.\\nHow it works\\nA cell\'s operation rests on an electronic component\'s ability to maintain one of two stable states, interpreted as a logical 0 or 1. For example, in dynamic random-access memory (DRAM) the cell is a capacitor: a charged state corresponds to 1, a discharged one to 0. The charge leaks away over time, however, so periodic refreshing is required. In static memory (SRAM) the cell is built from several transistors forming a flip-flop, which can hold its state stably without constant refreshing but takes up more space.\\nOrganisation and addressing\\nCells are organised into arrays (matrices), forming memory. To access a particular cell its address is used, which is decoded by the control circuitry. Modern processors usually access not individual bits but groups of cells (bytes, words) in one cycle, for efficiency.\\nAn example at a low level\\nConsider a simplified example in C, where working with memory is at its most visible. A variable in memory occupies one or more cells.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nThe concept of a memory cell is critically important in:\\n\\n- Processor architecture and memory design: it determines RAM\'s speed and density.\\n- Systems programming: understanding pointers, address arithmetic, and working with the stack and the heap.\\n- Optimisation: knowledge of data alignment and caching for efficient access.\\n- Electronics: designing memory chips and FPGAs.\\nSummary: Understanding the memory cell is essential for low-level programming, performance optimisation and a deep understanding of how a computer stores and processes information at the hardware level.","#include <stdio.h>\\n\\nint main() {\\n    // Declaring a variable \'a\' of type char (usually 1 byte = 8 bits).\\n    // Eight consecutive memory cells are allocated for it.\\n    char a = 65; // Decimal 65 in binary: 01000001\\n\\n    // Every bit of that value is held in a separate cell.\\n    // The variable\'s address is the address of the first cell of that group.\\n    printf(\\"The value: %d\\\\n\\", a);\\n    printf(\\"The address in memory: %p\\\\n\\", (void*)&a);\\n\\n    // Changing the value means changing the state of the corresponding cells.\\n    a = 66; // The binary representation is now: 01000010\\n    return 0;\\n}"],[1820,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight text format based on JavaScript\'s syntax, convenient for humans to read and simple for machines to parse. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is an extensible markup language using tags to structure data; it is more verbose but strict and supports schemas. CSV (Comma-Separated Values) is a simple text format for representing tabular data, where values are separated by commas or other delimiters; it is compact but does not support complex hierarchical structures.","The main differences between the data formats\\nJSON, XML and CSV are three widely used text formats for storing and transferring structured data. Each has its own area of application, syntax and characteristics.\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation)\\nJSON is a lightweight format based on a subset of JavaScript\'s syntax. It uses key-value pairs and ordered lists. Data is represented as objects (in curly braces) and arrays (in square brackets). JSON is easy for people to read and efficient for programs to process, especially in web development, where it has become the standard for APIs.\\n\\nXML (eXtensible Markup Language)\\nXML is a markup language that uses tags to define elements and attributes. It is more verbose than JSON, but strictly structured, and supports namespaces, schemas (XSD) and transformations (XSLT). XML is often used in enterprise systems, configuration files and wherever validating the data\'s structure matters.\\n\\nCSV (Comma-Separated Values)\\nCSV is the simplest format for representing tabular data. Every line of the file corresponds to a row of the table, and the values in a line are separated by commas (or other characters, for example semicolons). CSV does not support hierarchical structures or data types, but it is very compact and ideally suited to exporting/importing data in spreadsheets and simple databases.\\n\\nComparison and use\\n\\n- Readability and size: JSON is compact and easy to read. XML is verbose, but its structure is very visible. CSV is the most compact for tabular data, but its structure is flat.\\n- The complexity of the data: JSON and XML are excellent for nested, hierarchical data. CSV suits only flat tables.\\n- Validation and schemas: XML has powerful validation facilities through XSD. JSON can use JSON Schema, but that is less widespread. CSV has no standard mechanism for validating structure.\\n- Use: JSON dominates in web APIs and in modern applications\' configurations. XML is often encountered in enterprise and financial systems and in documents (for example, SOAP, RSS). CSV is used for data in tables, for reports and for simple exchange between programs such as Excel and databases.\\nThe choice of format depends on the task: use JSON for web services and configurations, XML when strict validation and complex markup are needed, and CSV for working with tabular data and maximum simplicity.","{\\n  \\"user\\": {\\n    \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n    \\"age\\": 30,\\n    \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n<user>\\n  <name>Alice</name>\\n  <age>30</age>\\n  <hobbies>\\n    <hobby>reading</hobby>\\n    <hobby>coding</hobby>\\n  </hobbies>\\n</user>\\n\\nname,age,hobby1,hobby2\\nAlice,30,reading,coding"],[1821,"Event-driven architecture is an approach where a program\'s flow is determined by events, such as user actions, messages from other systems or internal signals. The system\'s components interact by generating events and reacting to them, which makes it asynchronous and loosely coupled. That lets many simultaneous operations be handled efficiently, for example in web servers or microservices. The main advantages are scalability and responsiveness.","Event-driven architecture (EDA) is a paradigm for designing software systems in which the flow of execution is governed by events. Instead of traditional sequential execution of code, the system reacts to events that may occur at arbitrary moments in time. That makes the architecture ideal for asynchronous operations, where tasks run independently without blocking the main thread.\\n\\nThe key components\\n\\nEDA usually distinguishes several main elements:\\n\\n- The event producer: generates events, for example a user\'s click or a message from a sensor.\\n\\n- The event consumer: subscribes to events and handles them, performing particular logic.\\n\\n- The event channel: the mechanism for delivering events from the producer to the consumer, often through a message broker.\\n\\n- The event bus or message broker: the central component that routes the events, for example RabbitMQ, Kafka or Redis Pub/Sub.\\n\\nHow and where it is used\\n\\nEvent-driven architecture is widely used in areas requiring high scalability and responsiveness:\\n\\n- Web applications: handling HTTP requests in Node.js using the event loop.\\n\\n- Microservices: exchanging data between services through events, which reduces coupling.\\n\\n- IoT systems: processing streams of data from many devices in real time.\\n\\n- UI/UX: reacting to the user\'s actions in interfaces, for example in browsers or desktop applications.\\n\\nA code example in Node.js\\n\\nBelow is a simple example of using EventEmitter in Node.js to implement an event-driven approach:\\n\\nIn this example OrderService generates an \'orderPlaced\' event, to which several handlers react, performing different tasks asynchronously. That demonstrates loose coupling: OrderService knows nothing about the details of processing the payment or sending the email.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nEvent-driven architecture is especially useful when building high-load, distributed systems that require asynchronous processing and scalability. It is worth applying when a system\'s components have to interact without a rigid dependency, for example in microservice applications, in real time or in complex user interfaces.","const EventEmitter = require(\'events\');\\n\\n// Create an EventEmitter instance\\nclass OrderService extends EventEmitter {\\n  placeOrder(order) {\\n    console.log(`Order placed: ${order.id}`);\\n    // Emit the \'orderPlaced\' event\\n    this.emit(\'orderPlaced\', order);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst orderService = new OrderService();\\n\\n// Subscribe to the \'orderPlaced\' event\\norderService.on(\'orderPlaced\', (order) => {\\n  console.log(`Processing payment for order: ${order.id}`);\\n  // The payment logic could go here\\n});\\n\\norderService.on(\'orderPlaced\', (order) => {\\n  console.log(`Sending confirmation email for order: ${order.id}`);\\n  // Sending the email could go here\\n});\\n\\n// Simulate an order being placed\\norderService.placeOrder({ id: 123, item: \'Laptop\', amount: 999 });"],[1822,"An API (Application Programming Interface) interacts with a database through a dedicated data-access layer. That layer, often implemented with ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) libraries or drivers, translates requests from the API (for example, HTTP requests) into database commands (SQL queries). The API receives data from the client and validates it, then the data-access layer performs the operation (create, read, update, delete) and returns the result back to the API. Finally the API formats that result (for example, into JSON) and sends a response to the client.","An API (Application Programming Interface) acts as an intermediary between client applications (web browsers, mobile applications) and the database. Its main job is to accept requests, handle the business logic and manage the data without giving the client direct access to the database. That provides security, abstraction and centralised management of the rules for working with the data.\\n\\nThe data-access layer\\n\\nDirect interaction with the DBMS (database management system) happens through the data-access layer. That layer can be implemented in several ways:\\n\\n- Native drivers/clients: Using libraries for a particular DBMS directly (for example, `pg` for PostgreSQL, `mysql2` for MySQL).\\n\\n- ORM (Object-Relational Mapping): Libraries such as Sequelize (Node.js), SQLAlchemy (Python) or Hibernate (Java), which map the programming language\'s objects onto the database\'s tables.\\n\\n- Query builders: Tools such as Knex.js, which provide a programmatic interface for building SQL queries.\\n\\nA typical request flow\\n\\n- The client sends an HTTP request (for example, `POST /api/users` with a JSON body) to an API endpoint.\\n\\n- The API (a framework such as Express.js or Django REST Framework) routes the request to the appropriate controller.\\n\\n- The controller validates the incoming data and applies the business logic.\\n\\n- The controller calls a service or model method, which in turn uses the data-access layer.\\n\\n- The data-access layer builds and executes the appropriate SQL query (for example, `INSERT INTO users ...`) through the driver or the ORM.\\n\\n- The DBMS executes the query and returns the result (success, an error, a set of data) to the access layer.\\n\\n- The result is passed back to the API\'s controller.\\n\\n- The API formats the result (for example, into JSON), sets the correct HTTP status and sends a response to the client.\\n\\nA code example (Node.js + Express + the Sequelize ORM)\\n\\nConclusion: An API interacts with a database through an abstracting layer (an ORM, a driver), which lets the application\'s business logic be separated from the details of storing the data. This approach should be used to build secure, maintainable server applications that are independent of any particular DBMS.","// 1. The User model, defined through Sequelize, represents a table in the database\\nconst User = sequelize.define(\'User\', { name: DataTypes.STRING });\\n\\n// 2. The API endpoint for creating a user\\napp.post(\'/api/users\', async (req, res) => {\\n    try {\\n        // 3. The API receives the data from the client\\n        const { name } = req.body;\\n\\n        // 4. The business logic (for example, checking uniqueness)\\n        // ...\\n\\n        // 5. Interaction with the database through the ORM\\n        // Sequelize generates the SQL: INSERT INTO \\"Users\\" (\\"name\\") VALUES (?)\\n        const newUser = await User.create({ name });\\n\\n        // 6. The API formats and returns the result\\n        res.status(201).json({ id: newUser.id, name: newUser.name });\\n    } catch (error) {\\n        // Handling database errors (for example, a uniqueness violation)\\n        res.status(500).json({ error: \'Database operation failed\' });\\n    }\\n});"],[1823,"Cookies are transmitted in HTTP requests and responses through special headers. The server sends a cookie to the client using the `Set-Cookie` response header. The browser saves that cookie and automatically includes it in subsequent requests to the same domain using the `Cookie` header. That lets the server identify the user and maintain session state without changing the URL.","Cookies are small fragments of data that a server sends to a user\'s browser to be stored and then sent back with every request to the same domain. They are the fundamental mechanism for maintaining state in the inherently stateless HTTP protocol.\\n\\nHow the server sends cookies to the client\\n\\nThe server sets a cookie by adding a `Set-Cookie` header to the HTTP response. That header states the cookie\'s name, its value and various attributes, such as its lifetime (Expires or Max-Age), the domain, the path, and the Secure and HttpOnly flags.\\n\\nHow the client sends cookies back\\n\\nOn receiving a `Set-Cookie` header, the browser saves the name-value pairs stated. With every subsequent request to the domain whose path matches the cookie\'s Path attribute, the browser automatically adds a `Cookie` header to the request containing all the applicable cookies.\\n\\nThe key attributes and their effects\\n\\n- HttpOnly: Forbids access to the cookie through JavaScript (document.cookie), protecting against XSS attacks.\\n\\n- Secure: The cookie will be sent only over the secure HTTPS protocol.\\n\\n- SameSite: Controls whether the cookie is sent in cross-site requests (the values: Strict, Lax, None).\\n\\n- Domain and Path: Define the cookie\'s scope — which domains and paths on the server it is bound to.\\n\\nThis mechanism is widely used to manage user sessions (for example, to hold a session identifier), for personalisation (themes, language settings) and for analytics tracking.\\n\\nSummary: Cookies are transmitted through HTTP headers and are the main way of preserving state on the client side. They are worth using for authentication, for holding user preferences and for session data that has to persist between requests.","HTTP/1.1 200 OK\\nContent-Type: text/html\\nSet-Cookie: sessionId=abc123; Expires=Wed, 21 Oct 2025 07:28:00 GMT; HttpOnly; Path=/\\nSet-Cookie: userPref=darkMode; Max-Age=3600; Secure\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n...\\n\\nGET /dashboard HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\nCookie: sessionId=abc123; userPref=darkMode\\n..."],[1824,"A class is a template or blueprint describing the structure and behaviour of future entities. It defines the properties (fields) and the actions (methods). An object is a concrete instance created from that template, with its own unique property values. For example, the class \'Car\' describes the fact that a car has a colour and a model, while the object myCar is a particular red Tesla. Many objects can be created from a single class.","In object-oriented programming (OOP) a class and an object are fundamental but different concepts. A class serves as an abstract description defining the common characteristics and behaviour for a group of similar entities. An object is a concrete embodiment of that description in memory while the program runs.\\nA class as a blueprint\\nA class defines:\\n\\n- Attributes (fields or properties): the data that will be held (for example, a name, an age).\\n- Methods: functions describing the behaviour (for example, calculate the salary, print the information).\\nA class in itself occupies no memory for holding data — it merely sets the rules.\\nAn object as an instance\\nWhen you create an object (the process is called instantiation), memory is allocated for a particular set of data. Every object has its own values for the attributes defined in the class.\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nIn this example `Dog` is the class. `dog1` and `dog2` are objects (instances) of the `Dog` class. They have the same set of attributes (`name`, `age`) and methods (`bark`), but the attributes\' values differ.\\nWhere does this apply?\\nThis is the foundation of OOP, used in practically every modern language (Java, C#, Python, JavaScript ES6 and others) to model real-world entities, organise code, encapsulate data and reuse code through inheritance.\\nSummary: A class is an abstract type defining a structure. An object is a concrete entity created from that structure, with real data. Understanding this difference is critical for designing and writing clean, modular code.","# Define the class (the blueprint)\\nclass Dog:\\n    # The constructor - a method for initialising an object\\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\\n        self.name = name  # an attribute\\n        self.age = age    # an attribute\\n\\n    # A method (the behaviour)\\n    def bark(self):\\n        return f\\"{self.name} says woof!\\"\\n\\n# Create objects (instances) from the Dog blueprint\\ndog1 = Dog(\\"Rex\\", 5)   # The first object, with its own data\\ndog2 = Dog(\\"Bella\\", 3) # The second object\\n\\nprint(dog1.name)        # Output: Rex\\nprint(dog2.bark())      # Output: Bella says woof!\\nprint(dog1 is dog2)     # Output: False - these are different objects"],[1825,"Extending the existing user model is worthwhile when the new data relates logically and semantically to the user themselves and will be used in most scenarios involving them. For example, adding a \\"date of birth\\" or \\"phone number\\" field. You should create a new model (or table) when the data is a separate entity with its own logic, may be optional, has multiplicity (one user, many records) or requires isolation for performance. For example, order history or notification settings.","When designing or evolving an application, a dilemma often arises: should you add new fields to the existing user model or move them into a separate entity? The right choice affects the code\'s readability, performance and the system\'s future flexibility.\\nWhen to extend the existing model\\nThe main principle is that extension is appropriate when the new attributes are an inseparable, mandatory part of the user\'s description and are used in most operations involving them. That simplifies access to the data and maintains integrity.\\n\\n- Basic demographic data: date of birth, gender, country.\\n- Contact information: an additional email, a phone number (if there is only one).\\n- Default settings: the interface language, the time zone.\\nA code example demonstrating extension of the model:\\n\\nWhen to create a new model\\nCreating a separate entity is justified when the data forms a logically independent object, has a complex structure, or its link to the user is not rigid.\\n\\n- One-to-many relationships: order history, a list of delivery addresses, payment methods.\\n- Optional or rarely used data: an extended profile (hobbies, a biography), the settings for complex notifications.\\n- Data with its own life cycle: the user\'s sessions, authentication tokens.\\n- Performance considerations: large BLOBs (for example, a high-resolution avatar) can be moved out so as not to burden the frequent queries to the main table.\\nAn example of creating a separate model for addresses:\\n\\nConclusion: Extend the main model for mandatory, frequently used attributes that define the core of the \\"user\\" entity. Create new models for data with independent logic, multiple relationships, or when the database schema\'s performance and flexibility have to be optimised.","// Before\\nclass User {\\n  id: number;\\n  email: string;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\n// After the extension\\nclass User {\\n  id: number;\\n  email: string;\\n  name: string;\\n  phoneNumber: string; // The field added\\n  dateOfBirth: Date;   // The field added\\n  timezone: string;    // The field added\\n}\\n\\n// The main user model stays lightweight\\nclass User {\\n  id: number;\\n  email: string;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\n// A new entity for the addresses\\nclass UserAddress {\\n  id: number;\\n  userId: number; // The foreign key\\n  street: string;\\n  city: string;\\n  isDefault: boolean;\\n}\\n// One user can have several UserAddress records"],[1826,"Middleware is a function that has access to the request object (req), the response object (res) and the next middleware function in the application\'s processing cycle (next). In Express.js you write middleware by creating a function that takes those three arguments. Inside the function you perform the logic (for example, logging, authentication, parsing the request body) and then call next() to pass control to the next middleware, or you end the response by sending something to the client with res.send(). Your own middleware lets you handle common tasks for many routes centrally.","Middleware is a fundamental concept in web frameworks, especially in Node.js with Express. These are functions that run in a chain between receiving an HTTP request and sending a response. They let you encapsulate common logic, such as authentication, validation, logging or data compression, avoiding its duplication in every route handler.\\nHow middleware works\\nEvery middleware function receives three parameters: the request object (`req`), the response object (`res`) and the `next` function. The key idea is that middleware can either modify `req` and `res`, or end the cycle by sending a response, or pass control onwards by calling `next()`. If `next()` is not called, the request will hang.\\nA practical example of creating middleware\\nSuppose we need to log the method and URL of every incoming request. We create a function and register it at the application level, so that it works for every route.\\n\\nTypes of middleware and their use\\nMiddleware can be applied at different levels:\\n\\n- At the application level (through `app.use()`): it runs for every request.\\n- At the route level: it runs only for particular paths.\\n- At the router level: to group the logic of related routes.\\n- Error handling: special middleware with four arguments, `(err, req, res, next)`.\\nAn example of middleware for checking an API key for one particular route only:\\n\\nConclusion: Your own middleware is worth writing for any cross-cutting functionality that has to be applied to several routes. That keeps the code cleaner, observes the DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself) principle and lets you add or disable layers of processing easily, such as security, monitoring or data preparation.","// The middleware for logging\\nconst requestLogger = (req, res, next) => {\\n    const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();\\n    console.log(`[${timestamp}] ${req.method} ${req.originalUrl}`);\\n    // Important: we call next() to pass control onwards\\n    next();\\n};\\n\\n// Registering the middleware in an Express application\\nconst express = require(\'express\');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.use(requestLogger); // Applied to every route\\n\\napp.get(\'/\', (req, res) => {\\n    res.send(\'The home page\');\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);\\n\\nconst apiKeyMiddleware = (req, res, next) => {\\n    const apiKey = req.headers[\'x-api-key\'];\\n    if (apiKey === \'secret-key-123\') {\\n        next(); // The key is correct; we carry on\\n    } else {\\n        res.status(401).json({ error: \'Invalid API Key\' }); // We end the cycle\\n    }\\n};\\n\\napp.use(\'/api/admin\', apiKeyMiddleware); // We protect only the /api/admin/* routes"],[1827,"In Python variables hold not the objects themselves but references to them. When one variable is assigned to another, only the reference is copied, not the object. For mutable objects such as lists, that can lead to unexpected side effects if the object is changed through one variable — the changes will be visible through the other. For immutable objects such as numbers or strings, an operation that changes them creates a new object, so the references stay independent. The id() function returns an object\'s unique identifier, letting you check whether variables refer to the same object.","In Python all data is represented by objects, and variables are simply names (labels) referring to those objects. The reference mechanism means that on the assignment `a = b` the variable `a` starts pointing at the same object as `b` rather than a copy of it being created. This is fundamental to understanding a program\'s behaviour, especially when working with mutable data types.\\n\\nMutable and immutable objects\\n\\nObjects in Python divide into mutable and immutable. The immutable ones include numbers, strings and tuples. An attempt to change such an object creates a new one. The mutable ones include lists, dictionaries and sets — their contents can be changed in place.\\n\\nExamples of how references work\\n\\nConsider a simple example with an immutable object:\\n\\nNow an example with a mutable object — a list:\\n\\nPassing arguments into functions\\n\\nIn Python arguments are passed by assignment (call by assignment). That means a reference to the object is passed into the function. If the object is mutable, the function can modify it, which affects the original object outside the function.\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\nUnderstanding references is essential for:\\n\\n- Avoiding unexpected changes to data when working with mutable objects.\\n\\n- Optimising memory — several variables can refer to one large object without copies being created.\\n\\n- Correctly implementing functions that should, or should not, change their input data.\\n\\n- Working with collections, for example when using lists as functions\' default arguments (the well-known problem with mutable default arguments).\\n\\nConclusion: The reference mechanism in Python is the foundation of the language\'s data model. You need to understand it well in order to work correctly with mutable objects, avoid errors from unexpected changes to data, and write efficient code that does not create unnecessary copies of objects.","a = 10\\nb = a  # b refers to the same object, 10\\nprint(id(a) == id(b))  # True - the identifiers are the same\\n\\nb = 20  # b now refers to a new object, 20\\na = 30  # a refers to a new object, 30\\nprint(a, b)  # 30 20 - the changes are independent\\n\\nlist1 = [1, 2, 3]\\nlist2 = list1  # list2 refers to the same list\\nprint(id(list1) == id(list2))  # True\\n\\nlist2.append(4)  # we change the object through list2\\nprint(list1)  # [1, 2, 3, 4] - the change is visible through list1\\n\\n# To create an independent copy you have to use list1.copy() or list(list1)\\n\\ndef modify_list(lst):\\n    lst.append(\\"modified\\")  # changes the list passed in\\n\\ndef reassign_list(lst):\\n    lst = [\\"new\\"]  # creates a new local reference; it does not affect the outer object\\n\\nmy_list = [1, 2]\\nmodify_list(my_list)\\nprint(my_list)  # [1, 2, \\"modified\\"] - the list was changed\\n\\nreassign_list(my_list)\\nprint(my_list)  # [1, 2, \\"modified\\"] - unchanged"],[1828,"HTTP headers are key-value pairs transmitted in HTTP requests and responses to convey metadata. They control the connection\'s behaviour, for example stating the content type (Content-Type), the caching settings (Cache-Control) or the authentication data (Authorization). Headers help the client and the server agree on the data format, compression and other parameters of the exchange. Without them web communication would be far less flexible and efficient.","HTTP headers are an inseparable part of the HTTP protocol, consisting of textual metadata transmitted along with a request from the client (for example, a browser) or a response from the server. They do not contain the data of the request/response body itself (for example, an HTML page or JSON) but serve to control how that data should be handled.\\n\\nThe main categories of header\\n\\n- General headers: Applied both to requests and to responses (for example, `Cache-Control`, `Connection`).\\n\\n- Request headers: Contain information about the resource requested and about the client itself (for example, `User-Agent`, `Authorization`, `Accept`).\\n\\n- Response headers: Contain additional information about the server\'s response (for example, `Server`, `Set-Cookie`, `Location` for redirects).\\n\\n- Entity headers: Describe the content of the message body (for example, `Content-Type`, `Content-Length`).\\n\\nPractical use and examples\\n\\nHeaders are used to solve a great many problems. For example, the header `Content-Type: application/json` tells the client that the response body contains data in JSON format and should be parsed accordingly. The header `Cache-Control: max-age=3600` tells the browser the response can be cached for an hour.\\n\\nConsider an example of a simple HTTP request and response with headers:\\n\\nIn this example the client uses the `Accept` header to state that it expects a response in JSON format. In its response the server confirms that format with `Content-Type` and additionally sets a cookie through `Set-Cookie`.\\n\\nWhere and how they are used\\n\\nHeaders are used at every level of web development:\\n\\n- In browsers: To control caching, send credentials, state preferred languages.\\n\\n- In server applications (Node.js/Express, Python/Django and others): To set CORS policies (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`), compress data (`Content-Encoding`), manage sessions.\\n\\n- In APIs: For versioning (`Accept-Version`), pagination, token authentication (`Authorization`).\\n\\n- In proxies and CDNs: To control caching and transform requests.\\n\\nConclusion: Understanding HTTP headers and knowing how to work with them is critically important for building efficient, secure, correctly functioning web applications and APIs, since they are the main mechanism for controlling the context and behaviour of HTTP communication.","// An example HTTP GET request (client -> server)\\nGET /api/users HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\nUser-Agent: Mozilla/5.0\\nAccept: application/json\\nAuthorization: Bearer abc123token\\n\\n// An example HTTP response (server -> client)\\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\\nContent-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8\\nContent-Length: 85\\nCache-Control: no-cache\\nSet-Cookie: sessionId=xyz; Path=/\\n\\n{\\"users\\": [{\\"id\\": 1, \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\"}]}"],[1829,"To traverse cyclic structures such as linked lists or graphs, two main approaches are used. The first is the \\"tortoise and hare\\" (Floyd\'s) algorithm, which uses two pointers moving at different speeds to detect a cycle with no additional memory. The second is using a hash table or set to track the nodes already visited. These methods let you determine whether a structure contains a cycle and find where it begins.","Cyclic structures, such as linked lists with a loop or graphs with cycles, require special traversal algorithms to avoid looping forever and to process the data correctly. The main task is to determine whether a cycle exists and, if so, to find its beginning.\\nFloyd\'s algorithm (\\"the tortoise and the hare\\")\\nThis method uses two pointers that move through the structure at different speeds. The tortoise moves one node per step while the hare moves two. If there is a cycle in the structure, they will necessarily meet inside it. After they meet, you can find the cycle\'s beginning by resetting one of the pointers to the start and moving both at the same speed.\\n\\nUsing a set of visited nodes\\nA simpler approach is to hold the nodes visited in a hash table or a set. As you traverse each node, you check whether it has already been visited. If it has, a cycle has been detected. This method requires O(n) additional memory but is easy to implement and understand.\\n\\nThese approaches are used in tasks that analyse linked lists, traverse graphs, detect infinite loops in simulations or process dependencies (for example, in build systems).\\nConclusion: Floyd\'s algorithm is memory-efficient and is often used in constrained environments, while the method with a set is simpler to understand and debug. The choice depends on the memory requirements and on the complexity of the implementation.","function hasCycle(head) {\\n  let slow = head;\\n  let fast = head;\\n  while (fast && fast.next) {\\n    slow = slow.next;\\n    fast = fast.next.next;\\n    if (slow === fast) {\\n      return true; // A cycle was detected\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return false; // There is no cycle\\n}\\n\\nfunction hasCycleSet(head) {\\n  let visited = new Set();\\n  let current = head;\\n  while (current) {\\n    if (visited.has(current)) {\\n      return true;\\n    }\\n    visited.add(current);\\n    current = current.next;\\n  }\\n  return false;\\n}"],[1830,"The main ways are a strict specification of the requirements, writing unit tests, carrying out code review and using static code analysis. Decomposing complex algorithms into simple, verifiable functions also helps. Applying formal methods, such as contract programming or verification tools, significantly reduces the risk of logical errors.","Deterministic algorithms that do not depend on external state (memory) must always produce the same result for the same input. Minimising errors in them is the key to building a reliable core for an application.\\nThe main approaches to improving reliability\\n\\n- A clear specification: Before writing the code you have to describe formally what exactly the algorithm should do, including the edge cases.\\n- Unit testing: Covering every possible path of execution, including invalid input, helps you find errors at an early stage.\\n- Code review: A view from outside lets you find logical errors the author may have missed.\\n- Static analysis: Using linters and static code analysers to spot potential bugs and anti-patterns.\\nPractical examples and techniques\\nConsider a function that computes a factorial. An error can arise when handling zero or negative numbers.\\n\\nAnother powerful method is decomposition. A complex sorting or searching algorithm is broken into small functions, each of which is easy to test and check.\\nFormal methods and tools\\nFor critically important systems, stricter approaches are used: contract programming (pre/postconditions, invariants), using languages with an advanced type system (for example, Haskell, Rust) or even formal verification tools (for example, TLA+ for checking algorithms).\\nConclusion: A combination of a clear specification, automated testing, team code review and, where justified, formal methods is the best way to minimise errors in deterministic algorithms, which matters especially in financial calculations, compiler cores or cryptography.","// An example in JavaScript with contracts as comments\\n/**\\n * Computes the factorial of a number.\\n * @param {number} n - A non-negative integer.\\n * @returns {number} The factorial of n.\\n * @throws {Error} If n < 0.\\n */\\nfunction factorial(n) {\\n  // The precondition\\n  if (n < 0) throw new Error(\'n must be non-negative\');\\n  // The loop invariant: result holds the factorial of the current i\\n  let result = 1;\\n  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\\n    result *= i;\\n  }\\n  // The postcondition: result == n!\\n  return result;\\n}\\n\\n// A unit test for this function\\n// assert(factorial(0) === 1);\\n// assert(factorial(5) === 120);"],[1831,"A FIFO (First In, First Out) queue is a data structure where the first task added is processed first. It can be implemented with an array, using push to add and shift to remove. For asynchronous processing with controlled concurrency, a pattern with a loop is often used that takes tasks from the queue and runs them with a limit on how many work at once. That guarantees the order of processing and prevents the system being overloaded.","A FIFO (First In, First Out) queue is a basic data structure that models the behaviour of an ordinary queue, for example in a shop: whoever arrived first is served first. In programming such a queue is often used to manage tasks that have to run in the order they arrive, especially in asynchronous scenarios such as handling API requests, background computation or managing streams of data.\\n\\nA basic implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nThe simplest queue can be built on an array, using push (adding at the end) and shift (removing from the front). For efficiency with a large number of elements, however, it is better to use your own implementation based on a linked list, to avoid the cost of shifting the indices during a shift operation.\\n\\nAsynchronous task processing with a concurrency limit\\n\\nIn practice tasks often require asynchronous execution, for example network requests. So as not to overload the system, you can implement a handler that takes tasks from the queue and runs them with a limit on how many work at once (a concurrency limit).\\n\\nWhere a FIFO queue is used\\n\\n- Message processing: in systems with message brokers (RabbitMQ, Kafka) the order often matters.\\n\\n- Managing requests: rate limiting an API, where requests are queued.\\n\\n- Animations and the UI: running animations or interface updates in sequence.\\n\\n- Background tasks: handling file uploads or sending emails in web applications.\\n\\nConclusion: A FIFO queue is worth applying when you need to guarantee the order in which tasks are processed and to control the load on the system, especially in asynchronous scenarios with limited resources.","class FIFOQueue {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.items = [];\\n  }\\n\\n  enqueue(item) {\\n    this.items.push(item);\\n  }\\n\\n  dequeue() {\\n    return this.items.shift();\\n  }\\n\\n  isEmpty() {\\n    return this.items.length === 0;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass AsyncTaskQueue {\\n  constructor(concurrency = 1) {\\n    this.queue = [];\\n    this.concurrency = concurrency;\\n    this.running = 0;\\n  }\\n\\n  addTask(task) {\\n    this.queue.push(task);\\n    this.process();\\n  }\\n\\n  async process() {\\n    while (this.running < this.concurrency && this.queue.length) {\\n      const task = this.queue.shift();\\n      this.running++;\\n      task().finally(() => {\\n        this.running--;\\n        this.process();\\n      });\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of use\\nconst queue = new AsyncTaskQueue(2);\\nqueue.addTask(() => fetch(\'/api/data1\'));\\nqueue.addTask(() => fetch(\'/api/data2\'));"],[1832,"Python has no strict access modifiers such as private or protected, as Java does. A naming convention is used instead: an attribute with a single leading underscore (for example, `_value`) is considered protected and serves as a signal to developers. An attribute with two leading underscores (for example, `__secret`) triggers the \\"name mangling\\" mechanism, which distorts the name to make accidental access from outside harder, but does not make it fully private. That reflects Python\'s philosophy — \\"we are all adults here\\": developers are trusted to access the internals when necessary.","Unlike languages with strict encapsulation, such as Java, C++ or C#, Python uses a more flexible, trusting approach to controlling access to a class\'s attributes and methods. There are no `private`, `protected` or `public` keywords in their classic sense here. Naming conventions and one special name-translation mechanism are used instead.\\n\\nThe naming conventions\\n\\n- A single underscore (for example, `_internal_value`): This is a signal to the developer that the attribute or method is intended for internal use within the class or its subclasses. The Python interpreter does not block access to such an attribute from outside. It is the analogue of `protected` in other languages.\\n\\n- Two underscores (for example, `__private_value`): Triggers the name mangling mechanism. The interpreter changes the attribute\'s name inside the class by adding a `_ClassName` prefix to it. That makes accidental access, or overriding the attribute in subclasses, harder, but does not make it completely inaccessible.\\n\\n- No underscore: The public interface, accessible from anywhere.\\n\\nA code example with name mangling\\n\\nWhere and how this applies\\n\\nThis approach is used to create a clear public API for a class, hiding the implementation details. The name mangling mechanism is useful for avoiding name conflicts in large inheritance hierarchies, especially in libraries and frameworks. Developers can access \\"protected\\" attributes when debugging, inheriting or creating mixins, which gives greater flexibility.\\n\\nConclusion: Python prefers conventions and trust in the developer to rigid restrictions. Use a single underscore for internal attributes and two when you need to avoid name conflicts in subclasses, understanding that there is no complete privacy in the classic sense.","class MyClass:\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.public = \\"I am public\\"\\n        self._protected = \\"I am a hint\\"\\n        self.__private = \\"I am mangled\\"\\n\\nobj = MyClass()\\nprint(obj.public)          # Works\\nprint(obj._protected)      # Works, but is not recommended\\n# print(obj.__private)     # Raises an AttributeError\\n\\n# Access to the \\"private\\" attribute through the mangled name:\\nprint(obj._MyClass__private)  # Prints: I am mangled"],[1833,"Dynamic typing lets polymorphism be implemented through duck typing, where objects are considered on the basis of their behaviour (the presence of methods) rather than an explicit type. That makes the code more flexible, since polymorphic operations can work with any object supporting the interface required, without inheriting from a common class. It can lead to runtime errors, however, if an object does not implement the expected method, and it complicates static analysis of the code.","Dynamic typing means that variables\' types are checked while the program runs rather than at compile time. That directly affects how polymorphism — the ability of objects with different internal structures to be handled through a single interface — is implemented in a language.\\nDuck typing as the foundation\\nIn dynamically typed languages (for example, Python, JavaScript, Ruby) polymorphism is often implemented through the duck typing mechanism. The principle states: \\"If something walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, then it is a duck.\\" That is, an object is considered suitable for an operation if it has the methods or attributes required, regardless of its explicit type or class hierarchy.\\nAn example in Python\\nConsider a function that works with any object having a `quack` method.\\n\\nThe `make_it_quack` function is polymorphic: it accepts any object that has a `quack` method. No common base class or explicit interface is required.\\nAdvantages and disadvantages\\n\\n- Flexibility: The code can work with types not foreseen, which is convenient for prototyping and scripts.\\n- Simplicity: There is no need to declare complex hierarchies of interfaces.\\n- The risk of runtime errors: If you pass an object with no `quack` method, the program crashes with an exception (for example, an `AttributeError` in Python).\\n- Difficulty of analysis: It is harder for static analysis tools and for developers to understand which types are acceptable.\\nComparison with static typing\\nIn statically typed languages (for example, Java, C#) polymorphism is usually achieved through inheritance and explicit interfaces, which provides type safety at compile time. Dynamic typing shifts that check to runtime, sacrificing safety for expressiveness and brevity.\\nConclusion: Dynamic typing makes polymorphism more implicit and flexible through duck typing, which suits scenarios where development speed and working with heterogeneous data matter, but requires careful testing to avoid runtime errors.","class Duck:\\n    def quack(self):\\n        return \\"Quack!\\"\\n\\nclass Person:\\n    def quack(self):\\n        return \\"I am quacking like a duck!\\"\\n\\ndef make_it_quack(entity):\\n    # Dynamic polymorphism: only the presence of the method is checked\\n    print(entity.quack())\\n\\n# Both objects work, although they are from different classes\\nduck = Duck()\\nperson = Person()\\nmake_it_quack(duck)   # Output: Quack!\\nmake_it_quack(person) # Output: I am quacking like a duck!"],[1834,"Step counters in traversal algorithms are used to track the number of iterations performed or nodes visited. That helps you control the depth of the search, prevent infinite loops in graphs with cycles and analyse the algorithm\'s performance. For example, in BFS or DFS you can limit the maximum depth of the traversal. Counters are also used for debugging and for logging the traversal process.","Step counters are simple variables (for example, `steps` or `visitedCount`) incremented on every iteration of a traversal algorithm\'s main loop. Their main purpose is to provide a quantitative metric of the algorithm\'s progress.\\nThe main uses of step counters\\n\\n- Controlling the depth of the traversal: In recursive algorithms such as DFS, a counter can limit the depth of the recursion, preventing a stack overflow or a descent into an \\"infinite\\" branch.\\n- Preventing looping: When traversing graphs with cycles (where nodes are not marked as visited), a counter can be used as a safeguard: if the number of steps exceeds a sensible limit (for example, the number of nodes), the algorithm aborts.\\n- Analysing complexity: Counting the steps lets you assess an algorithm\'s time complexity empirically on particular data.\\n- Debugging and logging: A counter helps you print intermediate states, which makes tracing the flow of execution easier.\\nA practical example: BFS with a step counter\\nConsider an implementation of breadth-first search (BFS) for a graph represented as an adjacency list, with a counter of the iterations performed added.\\n\\nIn this example `step_counter` is increased every time we take a node from the queue. That corresponds to the number of nodes processed. In real scenarios such a counter can be used to abort the traversal if the number of steps becomes suspiciously large, which may indicate a logical error in the graph\'s structure.\\nWhere this is used\\nStep counters are often encountered in pathfinding algorithms (A*, Dijkstra), in tree traversals (iterative DFS), and in various competitive-programming tasks where limiting the execution time matters. In web crawlers a counter can limit the depth of nesting of the pages scanned.\\nConclusion: Use step counters in traversal algorithms to add control over the execution, to debug and to obtain performance metrics, especially when working with data of unknown structure or size.","from collections import deque\\n\\ndef bfs_with_counter(graph, start):\\n    visited = set()\\n    queue = deque([start])\\n    visited.add(start)\\n    step_counter = 0  # Initialising the counter\\n    \\n    while queue:\\n        step_counter += 1\\n        node = queue.popleft()\\n        print(f\\"Step {step_counter}: Visiting node {node}\\")\\n        \\n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\\n            if neighbor not in visited:\\n                visited.add(neighbor)\\n                queue.append(neighbor)\\n    \\n    print(f\\"Total steps (iterations): {step_counter}\\")\\n    return step_counter\\n\\n# An example graph\\ngraph = {\\n    \'A\': [\'B\', \'C\'],\\n    \'B\': [\'A\', \'D\', \'E\'],\\n    \'C\': [\'A\', \'F\'],\\n    \'D\': [\'B\'],\\n    \'E\': [\'B\', \'F\'],\\n    \'F\': [\'C\', \'E\']\\n}\\n\\nbfs_with_counter(graph, \'A\')"],[1835,"To be sure a traversal was complete you need to track every node visited. A visited set is used for that, into which each node is added on its first visit. Once the algorithm finishes, the number of nodes visited is compared with the total number of nodes in the structure. If they are equal, the traversal was complete. For trees you can also check that every node was processed exactly once.","A complete traversal of a data structure such as a graph or a tree means the algorithm visited every node (vertex) at least once. That is a fundamental requirement for many algorithms, including searching, analysing connectivity or computing a graph\'s properties.\\nThe main approach: tracking the nodes visited\\nThe most widespread way is to use a data structure to hold the nodes already processed. That is usually a hash set (a visited set), which provides fast checking and insertion. Every time a new node is visited, its identifier is added to the set. If a node is already in the set, it is not processed again, which prevents looping in cyclic graphs.\\nThe practical check after the traversal\\nOnce the traversal algorithm (for example, BFS or DFS) has finished, you have to compare the size of the set of visited nodes with the known total number of nodes in the structure. If those numbers match, the traversal can be considered complete. In situations where the total number of nodes is not known in advance (for example, when traversing a graph given only as an adjacency list), a complete traversal often means the algorithm visited every node reachable from the starting point. To check that the whole structure was traversed in that case, you may have to run the algorithm from every unvisited vertex (for disconnected graphs).\\nA code example for a graph in Python\\n\\nIn this example the function implements BFS and checks whether the traversal reached every node of the graph. Since the node \'F\' is isolated and not connected to the starting node \'A\', the function returns False. To traverse such a disconnected graph completely you would have to run the algorithm from every node not yet visited.\\nConclusion\\nChecking the completeness of a traversal is necessary when testing algorithms, working with data of unknown structure or analysing connectivity. Use a visited set for tracking and compare the sizes at the end of execution. For disconnected graphs, implement an outer loop that starts a traversal from every unvisited vertex, to guarantee complete coverage.","from collections import deque\\n\\ndef is_full_traversal(graph, start_node):\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    graph: a dict, the adjacency-list representation.\\n    start_node: the node to start the traversal from.\\n    Returns True if the traversal reached every node of the graph.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    if not graph:\\n        return True\\n    visited = set()\\n    queue = deque([start_node])\\n    visited.add(start_node)\\n    \\n    while queue:\\n        node = queue.popleft()\\n        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):\\n            if neighbor not in visited:\\n                visited.add(neighbor)\\n                queue.append(neighbor)\\n    # Compare the number of nodes visited with the total number of nodes in the graph\\n    return len(visited) == len(graph)\\n\\n# An example graph\\nsample_graph = {\\n    \'A\': [\'B\', \'C\'],\\n    \'B\': [\'A\', \'D\'],\\n    \'C\': [\'A\', \'E\'],\\n    \'D\': [\'B\'],\\n    \'E\': [\'C\'],\\n    \'F\': []  # An isolated node\\n}\\n# For start_node=\'A\' the traversal will not be complete, since the node F is unreachable.\\nprint(is_full_traversal(sample_graph, \'A\'))  # Output: False"],[1836,"A bidirectional traversal is a search algorithm that runs two searches at once: one from the starting point, the other from the end. They move towards each other until they meet. That lets you reduce the number of vertices examined significantly compared with a classic breadth-first search (BFS), especially in large graphs. The main idea is to reduce the exponential growth of the search space. This approach is effective when both the starting and the target vertex are known.","Bidirectional search is a powerful optimisation technique for graph-traversal and pathfinding problems that is especially effective in undirected graphs when both the start and the target vertex are known. Instead of exploring the whole space from the starting point to the goal, the algorithm runs two parallel searches: one \\"forwards\\" from the start, the other \\"backwards\\" from the goal. The searches continue until their frontiers intersect.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nThe algorithm maintains two queues (if BFS is used) and two sets of visited vertices — one for each direction. At every step it expands a vertex from the smaller queue (or takes turns), checking whether the current vertex has appeared in the opposite search\'s set of visited vertices. If it has, the path has been found. The path is built by joining the path from the start to the meeting point with the path from the meeting point to the goal (in reverse order).\\n\\nAdvantages and uses\\n\\n- Efficiency in time and memory: In the worst case the complexity remains O(b^(d/2)), where b is the branching factor and d the depth of the goal. That is the square root of ordinary BFS\'s complexity (O(b^d)), which gives a huge win for large graphs.\\n\\n- Typical scenarios: Finding the shortest path in undirected graphs (for example, in social networks to find the connections between people), solving puzzles (the 15 puzzle), checking connectivity in very large graphs.\\n\\nA code example (a simplified BFS)\\n\\nConclusion: A bidirectional traversal is worth applying when the graph is large and undirected and clear start and end points are known. It radically reduces the search space, saving computational resources, and is an excellent example of optimising fundamental algorithms.","function bidirectionalBFS(graph, start, target) {\\n    if (start === target) return [start];\\n\\n    // The queues and visited nodes for the two directions\\n    let queueStart = [start];\\n    let queueTarget = [target];\\n    let visitedStart = new Map(); // vertex -> parent\\n    let visitedTarget = new Map();\\n    visitedStart.set(start, null);\\n    visitedTarget.set(target, null);\\n\\n    while (queueStart.length && queueTarget.length) {\\n        // Expand the search from the start\\n        let meetingNode = expandLevel(graph, queueStart, visitedStart, visitedTarget);\\n        if (meetingNode) return constructPath(meetingNode, visitedStart, visitedTarget);\\n\\n        // Expand the search from the goal\\n        meetingNode = expandLevel(graph, queueTarget, visitedTarget, visitedStart);\\n        if (meetingNode) return constructPath(meetingNode, visitedStart, visitedTarget);\\n    }\\n    return null; // No path was found\\n}\\n\\nfunction expandLevel(graph, queue, visitedFrom, visitedOpposite) {\\n    let vertex = queue.shift();\\n    for (let neighbor of graph[vertex]) {\\n        if (!visitedFrom.has(neighbor)) {\\n            visitedFrom.set(neighbor, vertex);\\n            // Checking for the meeting\\n            if (visitedOpposite.has(neighbor)) return neighbor;\\n            queue.push(neighbor);\\n        }\\n    }\\n    return null;\\n}\\n// The constructPath function assembles the final path from the two halves."],[1837,"A system\'s state, for example a light (on/off), can be used to store one bit of information. A light that is on can mean 1, one that is off 0. This is the fundamental principle behind every digital device, where physical states (voltage, magnetisation) encode data. Even a simple switch thus becomes an elementary memory cell.","The concept of using a physical state to store information underlies the whole of computing. Any device capable of being in two or more stable states can serve as an elementary memory cell. The simplest example is an ordinary light switch.\\n\\nThe bit as the unit of information\\n\\nOne such state (for example, \\"the light is on\\") can represent a logical one (1), and the opposite state (\\"the light is off\\") a logical zero (0). That pair of values makes up one bit — the minimal unit of information in the digital world. A group of bits (a byte is 8 bits) lets more complex data be encoded: numbers, letters, instructions.\\n\\nFrom physics to logic\\n\\nIn real electronic devices it is not light bulbs that are used but other physical phenomena:\\n\\n- Transistors in a processor: The presence or absence of a voltage on a transistor\'s gate determines whether it is open or closed, which corresponds to 1 or 0.\\n\\n- Random-access memory (DRAM): The information is held as a charge in a microscopic capacitor. A charged capacitor is 1, a discharged one 0.\\n\\n- Hard disks (HDDs): The magnetisation of microscopic areas on the disk is used. The direction of the magnetic field encodes 0 or 1.\\n\\n- Flash memory (SSDs, USB): In flash memory\'s cells, electrons are trapped in a transistor\'s floating gate. The presence of a charge changes the threshold voltage, which is read as 0 or 1.\\n\\nA practical abstraction and an example\\n\\nIn programming we abstract away from the physical implementation and work with logical states directly. Consider an example in Python that imitates holding a lamp\'s state and using it to make a decision.\\n\\nSummary: Using a simple physical state to store a bit of information is the cornerstone of digital technology. This principle is applied everywhere: from the flip-flops in processors to the gigabytes of memory in your smartphone. Understanding this link between the physical world and logical data is critically important for developing hardware and low-level software.","# A class representing the simplest state-based memory cell\\nclass LightSwitch:\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.is_on = False  # The initial state: off (0)\\n\\n    def turn_on(self):\\n        self.is_on = True   # Set the state to \\"on\\" (1)\\n\\n    def turn_off(self):\\n        self.is_on = False  # Set the state to \\"off\\" (0)\\n\\n    def get_state(self):\\n        # Returns the information held: 1 or 0\\n        return 1 if self.is_on else 0\\n\\n# Use\\nkitchen_light = LightSwitch()\\nprint(f\\"The initial state (the bit): {kitchen_light.get_state()}\\") # 0\\n\\n# We store the information \\"turn on\\"\\nkitchen_light.turn_on()\\nstored_bit = kitchen_light.get_state()\\nprint(f\\"The bit stored: {stored_bit}\\") # 1\\n\\n# We use the information stored to make a decision\\nif stored_bit == 1:\\n    print(\\"The action: the room is lit, you can read.\\")\\nelse:\\n    print(\\"The action: the room is dark, the light has to be switched on.\\")"],[1838,"To determine the number of elements in a cyclic structure without markers you can use Floyd\'s algorithm (\\"the tortoise and the hare\\"). First find the meeting point of two pointers moving at different speeds, to detect the cycle. Then, holding one pointer still, count how many steps the second needs to return to the same point — that gives the cycle\'s length. Finally, to find the total number of elements, including the tail before the cycle, you can reset one pointer to the start and move both at the same speed until they meet.","Cyclic data structures, such as linked lists with loops, are often encountered in programming, and determining their size (the total number of unique elements) is a non-trivial task, since a simple traversal would loop forever. Floyd\'s cycle-detection algorithm (\\"the tortoise and the hare\\") provides an elegant solution.\\nDetecting the cycle and its length\\nThe algorithm uses two pointers: a slow one (moving 1 step per iteration) and a fast one (2 steps). If there is a cycle in the structure, they will necessarily meet inside it. Once they have met, you can hold one pointer still and count how many steps the second needs to go round the cycle and return to the same point — that gives the cycle\'s length (L).\\nDetermining the structure\'s total size\\nTo find the total number of elements (N), including the tail before the entry into the cycle, you have to determine the point where the cycle begins. Having found the cycle\'s length, reset one pointer to the start of the structure and leave the other at the meeting point. Now move both pointers at the same speed (1 step each). They will meet precisely at the cycle\'s entry point. Knowing the tail\'s length (K) and the cycle\'s length (L), the total size is N = K + L.\\nA code example in Python\\n\\nThis approach is efficient in time (O(N)) and memory (O(1)), since it uses only two pointers. It is used when analysing data structures, debugging algorithms and in graph-related problems.\\nConclusion: Floyd\'s algorithm is worth applying when you need to analyse cyclic structures without additional memory, for example when working with linked lists, graphs or streams of data where looping is possible.","class Node:\\n    def __init__(self, value):\\n        self.value = value\\n        self.next = None\\n\\ndef count_elements_in_cyclic_list(head):\\n    if not head:\\n        return 0\\n    # Step 1: detecting the cycle with Floyd\'s algorithm\\n    slow = fast = head\\n    has_cycle = False\\n    while fast and fast.next:\\n        slow = slow.next\\n        fast = fast.next.next\\n        if slow == fast:\\n            has_cycle = True\\n            break\\n    # If there is no cycle, we simply count the elements\\n    if not has_cycle:\\n        count = 0\\n        current = head\\n        while current:\\n            count += 1\\n            current = current.next\\n        return count\\n    # Step 2: determining the cycle\'s length (L)\\n    cycle_length = 1\\n    fast = slow.next\\n    while fast != slow:\\n        cycle_length += 1\\n        fast = fast.next\\n    # Step 3: finding the entry point and the tail\'s length (K)\\n    slow = head\\n    fast = head\\n    # Move fast L steps forward\\n    for _ in range(cycle_length):\\n        fast = fast.next\\n    # Move both at the same speed\\n    tail_length = 0\\n    while slow != fast:\\n        slow = slow.next\\n        fast = fast.next\\n        tail_length += 1\\n    # The total size: the tail plus the cycle\\n    return tail_length + cycle_length\\n\\n# An example of use\\n# We build the list: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 3 (a cycle back to element 3)\\nnode1 = Node(1); node2 = Node(2); node3 = Node(3); node4 = Node(4); node5 = Node(5)\\nnode1.next = node2; node2.next = node3; node3.next = node4; node4.next = node5; node5.next = node3\\nprint(count_elements_in_cyclic_list(node1))  # Output: 5 (the elements 1,2,3,4,5)"],[1839,"Removing an element from a collection (for example, an ArrayList) directly while iterating with a for-each loop or an iterator raises a ConcurrentModificationException. For safe removal use the Iterator object\'s own `remove()` method, which removes the current element and updates the iterator\'s state correctly. Alternatively you can collect the elements to be removed into a separate list and remove them after the loop, or use `removeIf()` (in Java 8+) or iterate by index in reverse order for lists.","Removing elements from a collection while iterating is a frequent task, for example when filtering data. Removing directly through the collection\'s methods (for example, `list.remove(element)`) inside a for-each loop, or while an iterator is active, breaks the iterator\'s internal state, which leads to a `ConcurrentModificationException` in Java or undefined behaviour in other languages.\\nThe safe approaches\\nThe main ways of removing safely:\\n\\n- Using Iterator.remove(): The most direct and recommended way. The iterator provides a `remove()` method that removes the current element (the last one returned by `next()`) and keeps the collection\'s and the iterator\'s state in sync.\\n- Collecting the elements to remove: Create a temporary list of the elements that need removing and perform the removal after the iteration finishes.\\n- Using removeIf() (Java 8+): A convenient collection method that takes a predicate.\\n- Iterating by index in reverse order: For lists (for example, an ArrayList) you can iterate from the end, so that the shifting of indices during removal does not affect the subsequent elements.\\nCode examples\\nAn example with `Iterator.remove()` in Java:\\n\\nAn example with `removeIf()`:\\n\\nAn example of reverse iteration by index for an ArrayList:\\n\\nConclusion: Use `Iterator.remove()` for safe removal while iterating over any collection; for lists, reverse iteration by index also works, and `removeIf()` is the most concise option when you have a predicate.","List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\\"a\\", \\"b\\", \\"c\\"));\\nIterator<String> it = list.iterator();\\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\\n    String item = it.next();\\n    if (item.equals(\\"b\\")) {\\n        it.remove(); // Safe removal\\n    }\\n}\\n// list is now [\\"a\\", \\"c\\"]\\n\\nlist.removeIf(item -> item.equals(\\"b\\"));\\n\\nfor (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\\n    if (list.get(i).equals(\\"b\\")) {\\n        list.remove(i); // Safe, since the indices shift only for the elements already processed\\n    }\\n}"],[1840,"Creating a string with a literal (for example, \'hello\') returns a primitive string value. Creating it with `new String(\'hello\')` returns a wrapper object (a String object). Primitives are lighter and are compared by value, while objects are compared by reference. String objects are rarely needed in practice, and using them can cause confusion in comparisons.","In JavaScript strings can be represented either as primitive values (string primitives) or as objects (String objects). Understanding the difference matters for working correctly with data types, for comparisons and for the code\'s efficiency.\\n\\nCreating one with a literal\\n\\nUsing a string literal (single, double or back quotes) creates a primitive string value.\\n\\nPrimitives are immutable, lightweight and compared by value. JavaScript automatically wraps a primitive in a String object when a method has to be called (for example, .toUpperCase()).\\n\\nCreating one with new String()\\n\\nUsing the `new String()` constructor creates a wrapper object (a String object).\\n\\nThis is a full object that holds the string value inside itself. Objects are compared by reference rather than by value, which can lead to surprises.\\n\\nThe key differences and examples\\n\\n- The data type: a literal is \\"string\\", new String is \\"object\\".\\n\\n- Comparison: primitives are compared by value, objects by reference.\\n\\n- Use: the string methods are available for primitives too, thanks to autoboxing. String objects are superfluous for most tasks.\\n\\n- Performance: primitives are more memory-efficient and faster in operations.\\n\\nWhere might new String() be useful?\\n\\nPractically never. Historically it might have been used to extend the prototype, or in very specific cases where working with an object explicitly was required. In modern code it is considered an anti-pattern.\\n\\nConclusion: Always use string literals to create strings. The `new String()` constructor can be misleading, complicate comparisons and worsen performance. Using it is justified only in exceptional situations, for example in low-level experiments with prototypes.","const strPrimitive = \'Hello World\'; // A primitive\\nconsole.log(typeof strPrimitive); // \\"string\\"\\n\\nconst strObject = new String(\'Hello World\'); // An object\\nconsole.log(typeof strObject); // \\"object\\"\\n\\nconst lit1 = \'test\';\\nconst lit2 = \'test\';\\nconsole.log(lit1 === lit2); // true (the same values)\\n\\nconst obj1 = new String(\'test\');\\nconst obj2 = new String(\'test\');\\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj2); // false (different objects in memory)"],[1841,"To turn a collection of objects into a collection of their fields\' values in JavaScript, the array method `map()` is used. Inside the callback passed to `map()` you return the value of the property you need from each object. For example, for an array of users, `users.map(user => user.name)` returns an array of names. This approach is clean and declarative, and creates a new array without changing the original.","Often, when working with data obtained from an API or a database, we have an array of objects (for example, users, products) and need to extract just one particular field from each object (for example, the identifier, the title, the email). That is needed to render lists, pass data into another component, or for simple aggregation.\\nUsing the map() method\\nThe standard and most widespread way in JavaScript is to use the built-in array method `map()`. It creates a new array by applying the function passed in to every element of the original array.\\n\\nAlternative approaches\\nAlthough `map()` is idiomatic, there are other ways, for example a `for...of` loop or the `forEach()` method. They are less declarative, however, and require more code to initialise the resulting array and add the elements explicitly.\\n\\nThe `map()` method is preferable, since it expresses the intention of transforming (mapping) one array into another explicitly and returns a new array, which conforms to the principles of functional programming (no side effects).\\nSpecial cases and performance\\nIf a collection may contain objects where the field you need is absent or equal to `undefined`, it is worth providing for such cases, for example with the optional chaining operator (`?.`) or a check.\\n\\nFor very large arrays in performance-critical places a simple `for` loop is sometimes considered, which can be slightly faster, but in the overwhelming majority of cases the difference is negligible and `map()`\'s readability matters more.\\nConclusion: Use the `map()` method to turn a collection of objects into an array of their fields\' values — this is the standard, clean, easily readable approach in JavaScript and TypeScript, ideally suited to preparing data for display in a UI or for further processing.","const users = [\\n  { id: 1, name: \'Alice\', age: 30 },\\n  { id: 2, name: \'Bob\', age: 25 },\\n  { id: 3, name: \'Charlie\', age: 35 }\\n];\\n\\n// Extracting the array of names\\nconst names = users.map(user => user.name);\\nconsole.log(names); // [\'Alice\', \'Bob\', \'Charlie\']\\n\\n// Extracting the array of ages\\nconst ages = users.map(user => user.age);\\nconsole.log(ages); // [30, 25, 35]\\n\\n// Using for...of\\nconst namesViaForOf = [];\\nfor (const user of users) {\\n  namesViaForOf.push(user.name);\\n}\\n\\n// Using forEach\\nconst namesViaForEach = [];\\nusers.forEach(user => namesViaForEach.push(user.name));\\n\\nconst mixedData = [\\n  { name: \'Alice\' },\\n  { age: 25 }, // there is no name field\\n  { name: \'Charlie\' }\\n];\\n\\nconst extractedNames = mixedData.map(item => item?.name);\\n// The result: [\'Alice\', undefined, \'Charlie\']\\n// To filter out the undefined values you can combine it with filter()\\nconst validNames = mixedData.map(item => item?.name).filter(name => name !== undefined);"],[1842,"Reactive programming is a paradigm oriented around streams of data and the propagation of changes. Libraries such as RxJava provide tools for working with asynchronous and event streams through Observables. They simplify the handling of events, network requests and background tasks, making code more readable and maintainable. That is especially useful in Android development for managing complex asynchronous operations.","Reactive programming is an approach to development that focuses on streams of data and the automatic propagation of changes. Instead of traditional imperative code, where you call methods explicitly, reactive libraries let you describe how data should be transformed and combined in streams. That is especially effective for asynchronous operations, such as network requests, user events or processing data in real time.\\n\\nThe key concepts\\n\\nIn RxJava the main building blocks are:\\n\\n- Observable: represents a stream of data or events that can be observed. It can emit zero or more items and then complete or raise an error.\\n\\n- Observer: subscribes to an Observable and handles the items emitted, the errors and the completion event.\\n\\n- Operators: methods such as map, filter and merge, which let you transform, combine or filter streams of data.\\n\\n- Schedulers: manage the threads of execution, letting you switch between, for example, the main thread and background threads.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nRxJava is widely used in Android development to simplify asynchronous tasks, such as:\\n\\n- Handling user input (for example, search autocomplete with a delay).\\n\\n- Performing network requests and handling the responses.\\n\\n- Managing the data\'s life cycle, to avoid memory leaks.\\n\\n- Composing several asynchronous operations without \\"callback hell\\".\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConsider a simple example in RxJava that performs a network request and handles the result:\\n\\nIn this example `fromCallable` creates an Observable from a task, `subscribeOn` states the thread to run on, and `observeOn` the thread on which to handle the result. That lets you avoid blocking the UI and simplifies error handling.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nReactive libraries such as RxJava are worth applying when you need to manage complex asynchronous streams, especially in applications with intensive user interaction or network operations. They improve the code\'s readability, make composing operations easier and help you avoid common problems such as memory leaks or a blocked interface.","Observable.fromCallable(() -> {\\n    // Simulating a network request\\n    return fetchDataFromNetwork();\\n})\\n.subscribeOn(Schedulers.io()) // Running on a background thread\\n.observeOn(AndroidSchedulers.mainThread()) // Handling it on the main thread\\n.subscribe(\\n    result -> {\\n        // Handling the data successfully\\n        updateUI(result);\\n    },\\n    error -> {\\n        // Handling the error\\n        showError(error);\\n    }\\n);"],[1843,"Access to an array element by index is fast because an array holds its elements in a contiguous block of memory. Any element\'s address is computed by a simple formula: the starting address plus (the index times the element\'s size). That is a fixed-time O(1) operation requiring no search. The computer does not walk through the elements but jumps straight to the memory cell required.","An array is a fundamental data structure that holds elements of the same type in a contiguous block of RAM. The key feature providing fast access is the contiguity of the layout and direct addressing.\\n\\nHow the address computation works\\n\\nWhen you declare an array, the system allocates one contiguous region of memory for it. Knowing that block\'s starting address (the address of the zeroth element) and the size of each element (for example, 4 bytes for a 32-bit integer), you can compute any element\'s address from its index instantly.\\n\\nThe formula looks like this:\\n\\nThat operation is performed in constant time, O(1). The computer does not need to look through the previous elements or perform complex computations — a single arithmetic operation on pointers is enough.\\n\\nAn example in C\\n\\nConsider a simple example illustrating the principle:\\n\\nComparison with other structures\\n\\nUnlike arrays, structures such as linked lists do not provide fast random access. To reach the Nth element of a list you have to walk through all the previous nodes, which takes O(n) time. Arrays, however, are ideal for scenarios requiring frequent reads of elements at known indices.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- Mathematical computation and algorithms: matrices, vectors, quicksort.\\n\\n- Caching data: thanks to the locality of the data, neighbouring elements are often loaded into the processor\'s cache, which speeds up processing.\\n\\n- I/O buffers: holding streams of data (audio, video).\\n\\n- Implementing other structures:\\n\\nSummary: Fast access by index is arrays\' main advantage, making them indispensable in tasks requiring intensive reading and modification of data at known positions. Use arrays when the speed of random access matters to you and the size of the data is fixed or changes rarely.","The_address_of_element[i] = The_starting_address + (i * The_size_of_an_element)\\n\\n#include <stdio.h>\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\\n    // Access to the element at index 2 (the third element)\\n    int value = arr[2]; // The address is computed: arr + 2 * sizeof(int)\\n    printf(\\"The element arr[2] = %d\\\\n\\", value); // Prints: 30\\n    return 0;\\n}"],[1844,"In the V8 engine the memory heap is divided into two generations: the young one (New Space) and the old one (Old Space). New objects are created in the young generation. During a garbage collection in the young generation (a Scavenge), the surviving objects are moved either into the other half of the young generation or, if they have survived several collections, into the old generation. A collection in the old generation (Mark-Sweep-Compact) happens more rarely and is slower. This is based on the generational hypothesis: most objects do not live long.","Garbage collection (GC) in JavaScript, specifically in the V8 engine, uses a generational collection strategy. That strategy optimises performance on the basis of the observation that most objects have a very short lifetime (the generational hypothesis).\\nThe structure of the memory heap\\nThe heap in V8 is divided into two main spaces:\\n\\n- The young generation (New Space): A small region of memory where new objects are created. It is divided into two equal halves: the \\"from-space\\" and the \\"to-space\\".\\n- The old generation (Old Space): A large region of memory for objects that have lived long enough.\\nThe process of moving objects\\nThe garbage collector works in several stages:\\n\\n- Creating an object: Any new object (for example, created with `{}` or `new`) is placed in the young generation\'s \\"from-space\\".\\n- Garbage collection in the young generation (Scavenge): When the \\"from-space\\" fills up, a fast Scavenge collection runs. It finds the live objects (those reachable from the roots) and copies them into the \\"to-space\\". Objects that have already survived one or more such collections are moved (\\"promoted\\") into the old generation. After the copying, the spaces swap roles: the \\"to-space\\" becomes the new \\"from-space\\".\\n- Garbage collection in the old generation (Mark-Sweep-Compact): A slower but more thorough algorithm is used to clear the old generation. It marks every reachable object, then removes (\\"sweeps\\") the unmarked ones and, if necessary, compacts the memory to avoid fragmentation.\\nA code example and observation\\nConsider a simple example illustrating the creation of short-lived and long-lived objects:\\n\\nIn practice a developer does not control this process directly, but understanding the mechanism helps you write code that minimises the load on the garbage collector (for example, by avoiding unnecessary memory allocations in performance-critical loops).\\nConclusion: The generational strategy lets V8 clear memory of numerous short-lived objects very quickly (frequent but fast Scavenge collections) and perform the costly clearing for long-lived objects more rarely. This knowledge is useful for optimising the performance of memory-intensive applications, such as games or complex Node.js web applications.","function createObjects() {\\n  // This object will most likely die quickly (collected in New Space)\\n  let tempObj = { data: \'temporary\' };\\n\\n  // This array may survive several collections and end up in Old Space\\n  let persistentArray = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    persistentArray.push({ index: i });\\n  }\\n  // persistentArray and its elements may be moved into Old Space\\n  return persistentArray;\\n}\\n\\nconst longLived = createObjects();\\n// After the function is called, tempObj is no longer reachable and will be removed.\\n// longLived (the array) is reachable and will end up in Old Space over time."],[1845,"The `volatile` keyword tells the compiler that a variable\'s value may change from outside (for example, by another thread or a device), so the compiler must not perform aggressive optimisations involving reads from and writes to that variable. It does not control the processor\'s caches directly, but it affects the order of memory operations, which can indirectly touch cache coherence. In multithreaded code, `volatile` in C/C++ does not guarantee atomicity or synchronisation; atomics or mutexes are needed for that.","The `volatile` keyword in languages such as C, C++, Java and C# is an instruction to the compiler, not to the processor. It states that a variable\'s value may change in a way invisible to the compiler (for example, by another thread, an interrupt handler or a hardware device). Its main purpose is to prevent compiler optimisations that could lead to incorrect program behaviour.\\n\\nThe effect on the compiler\'s optimisations\\n\\nWithout `volatile` the compiler may assume that a variable\'s value, not changed in the current thread, stays constant, and may cache it in a register or remove \\"unnecessary\\" reads and writes. With `volatile` the compiler is obliged to generate code that reads the variable from memory every time it is accessed and writes it to memory when it changes.\\n\\nThe connection with the processor\'s caches\\n\\nIt is important to understand that `volatile` inserts no hardware memory barriers and does not control the processor\'s caches directly. It affects only the order of read/write operations in the machine code generated. By guaranteeing that the memory operations really happen, however, it can indirectly affect cache coherence, since the processor sees the accesses to memory. For full control over the order of operations between threads and processors in C++11 and later you should use atomic operations (`std::atomic`) with a specified memory order (memory_order), which provide the necessary barriers.\\n\\nPractical use\\n\\n- Working with hardware: Devices\' registers in embedded systems, which change asynchronously.\\n\\n- Shared flags in multithreaded programs (with care): In Java `volatile` guarantees the visibility of changes between threads and forbids the reordering of operations, which is stronger than in C++.\\n\\n- Interrupt signals (interrupt handlers): Global variables changed in an interrupt handler.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `volatile` for variables that may change asynchronously outside the control of the current thread of execution, but do not rely on it to synchronise complex operations between threads — mutexes and atomics exist for that. It does not replace the memory barriers needed for a strict order of operations on multiprocessor systems.","// An example in C++\\nvolatile int flag = 0;\\n\\nvoid waitForFlag() {\\n    // Without volatile the compiler could optimise the loop into while(true),\\n    // since it does not see flag changing inside the function.\\n    while (flag == 0) {\\n        // Waiting\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Another thread or an interrupt can change flag to 1.\\n\\n// An example in Java\\npublic class SharedData {\\n    private volatile boolean ready = false;\\n\\n    public void writer() {\\n        // ... preparing the data\\n        ready = true; // The write is guaranteed to be visible to the reader\\n    }\\n\\n    public void reader() {\\n        while (!ready) { // The read is always from memory\\n            // Waiting\\n        }\\n        // ... using the data\\n    }\\n}"],[1846,"Stop-The-World (STW) is a pause in an application\'s work when every thread is suspended so that particular operations can be performed, most often garbage collection. During an STW the application does not respond to requests, which can cause delays. It is necessary to ensure the memory stays consistent while the garbage collector moves objects or updates references. Understanding STW helps you optimise applications by minimising such pauses.","Stop-The-World (STW) is a phase in a virtual machine\'s work (for example, the JVM for Java) when every thread of the application\'s execution is suspended. That happens because certain operations, first and foremost some stages of garbage collection (GC), require a consistent snapshot of the memory\'s state. If the threads carried on working, they could change objects or references while the collector was processing them, which would lead to corrupted data.\\n\\nWhy does Stop-The-World arise?\\n\\nThe main reason is garbage collection. For example, when the Mark-Sweep-Compact garbage-collection algorithm is used, the collector has to:\\n\\n- Mark every reachable object (the Mark phase).\\n\\n- Remove the unmarked objects (the Sweep phase).\\n\\n- Move the remaining objects to eliminate fragmentation (the Compact phase).\\n\\nThe Mark and especially the Compact phases require the threads to be suspended, to guarantee that references to objects do not change while they are being moved. Other operations, such as resizing the heap or certain virtual-machine operations (for example, deoptimising code), can also cause an STW.\\n\\nAn example of the effect on an application\\n\\nImagine a Java web service handling transactions. If a major GC occurs with a long STW pause (for example, 500 ms), every incoming HTTP request will hang during that time, increasing the response time. For real-time systems such as trading platforms or online games, even short pauses are unacceptable.\\n\\nHow do you minimise STW?\\n\\nModern garbage collectors use various strategies:\\n\\n- Low-pause collectors: For example, CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep) or G1 (Garbage-First) in Java perform part of the work concurrently with the application\'s threads, reducing the duration of the STW phases.\\n\\n- ZGC and Shenandoah: These collectors aim to reduce pauses to 10 ms and less by performing almost all the work concurrently.\\n\\n- Tuning the JVM\'s parameters: Increasing the heap\'s size and tuning the frequency of collections can help.\\n\\nA code example (simulating load)\\n\\nThis code constantly creates and discards large arrays, provoking activity from the garbage collector. By running it with the `-XX:+PrintGC` flag you can see the logs of the GC pauses.\\n\\nConclusion: Stop-The-World is an unavoidable compromise for ensuring the integrity of memory in managed runtimes. Understanding STW is critical for developing high-load applications, where you have to choose a suitable garbage collector and tune the parameters to minimise the pauses and keep the system responsive.","// Java code that creates load for the garbage collector\\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\npublic class STWSimulation {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        List<byte[]> list = new ArrayList<>();\\n        while (true) {\\n            // Create 1 MB of garbage on every iteration\\n            list.add(new byte[1024 * 1024]);\\n            try {\\n                Thread.sleep(10); // A small delay\\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) {\\n                e.printStackTrace();\\n            }\\n            // When the list grows large, the GC will cause an STW pause\\n            if (list.size() > 100) {\\n                list.clear(); // Release the references\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[1847,"Balancing a tree directly affects its height. In an unbalanced tree (for example, one degenerated into a list) the height can be O(n), which makes searching, insertion and deletion O(n) too. In a balanced tree (for example, an AVL or a red-black tree) the height is kept of the order of O(log n). That guarantees the complexity of searching will be logarithmic — O(log n) — which is significantly more efficient for large data sets.","A tree is a hierarchical data structure where every node has a value and references to child nodes. In the context of searching, for example in a binary search tree (BST), the operation of comparing a value with the root and descending recursively into the left or right subtree determines the efficiency.\\n\\nThe tree\'s height and the complexity of the operations\\n\\nThe key metric affecting the complexity of searching is the tree\'s height (the maximum number of edges from the root to the furthest leaf). In a perfectly balanced tree the nodes are distributed evenly at every level, which minimises the height.\\n\\n- In a balanced tree with n nodes the height h ≈ log₂(n).\\n\\n- In the worst case of an unbalanced tree (when every node has only one child) the height h = n-1.\\n\\nSince a search in a BST requires, in the worst case, a walk from the root to a leaf, its time complexity is directly proportional to the height.\\n\\nAn example: a balanced versus an unbalanced tree\\n\\nConsider two binary search trees with the same values, {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}.\\n\\nWhere are balanced trees used?\\n\\nBalanced trees (AVL, red-black, B-trees) are widely used in systems where predictable performance is critically important:\\n\\n- Databases and file systems (for indices).\\n\\n- Implementations of associative arrays (for example, TreeMap in Java).\\n\\n- Load balancers in network structures.\\n\\nConclusion: Balancing a tree guarantees a logarithmic search complexity of O(log n), which makes the operations efficient for large volumes of data. Use balanced trees when stable performance of searching, insertion and deletion is required, especially in dynamically changing data sets.","// An unbalanced tree (a degenerate list)\\n1\\n \\\\\\\\\\n  2\\n   \\\\\\\\\\n    3\\n     \\\\\\\\\\n      4\\n       \\\\\\\\\\n        5\\n// The height = 4; searching for 5 requires 4 comparisons.\\n\\n// A balanced tree (an example)\\n      3\\n     / \\\\\\\\\\n    2   4\\n   /     \\\\\\\\\\n  1       5\\n// The height ≈ 2; searching for 5 requires 2 comparisons."],[1848,"A class is not thread-safe if its internal state can be modified incorrectly by simultaneous access from several threads without external synchronisation. The main signs are: the presence of mutable (non-final) fields, the absence of synchronisation when shared data is accessed (for example, through synchronized or volatile in Java), and the use of unsafe data structures (for example, a plain ArrayList or HashMap instead of a ConcurrentHashMap). If a class is not documented as thread-safe, you should consider it unsafe and apply external locks.","A class\'s thread safety means its methods can be called from several threads at once with no risk of the data\'s integrity being broken or of an inconsistent state being obtained. You can determine that a class is not thread-safe from several key signs.\\nThe main signs of a lack of safety\\n\\n- Mutable state: The class contains non-final fields that can change after the object is created. If several threads read from and write to such fields without synchronisation, a race condition arises.\\n- The absence of internal synchronisation: The methods that change shared state have no synchronisation mechanisms (for example, the `synchronized` keyword in Java, `lock` in C# or mutexes in C++).\\n- A \\"leak\\" of a reference to the internal structures: A method returns a reference to an internal mutable object (for example, an array or a collection), letting external code change the class\'s state bypassing its methods, which breaks the encapsulation in a multithreaded environment.\\n- The use of unsafe components: The class relies on data structures not intended for multithreaded access (for example, `ArrayList` or `HashMap` in Java) without external synchronisation.\\n- The presence of compound operations: Methods that have to be performed atomically (for example, check-then-act) are broken into several steps with no synchronisation between them.\\nAn example of an unsafe class\\nConsider a simple counter in Java:\\n\\nThe `increment()` method performs the operation `value++`, which at the processor level consists of three steps: reading the value, increasing it, writing it. If two threads call that method at once, both may read the same old value, increase it and write it back. As a result the increment happens only once instead of twice.\\nHow to check in practice\\n\\n- Study the documentation: if a class is not marked `@ThreadSafe` and the documentation does not state that it is safe, assume the worst.\\n- Analyse the code: look for mutable fields, the absence of `synchronized` or `volatile`, or of the use of classes from `java.util.concurrent`.\\n- Carry out testing: run multithreaded tests with high contention and check whether discrepancies arise between the expected and the actual state.\\nConclusion: A class is not thread-safe if its internal state can be corrupted by concurrent access. Such a class cannot be used in a multithreaded environment without external synchronisation. To make it safe you have to either make the state immutable or use locks, atomic types or thread-safe collections.","public class UnsafeCounter {\\n    private int value = 0;\\n\\n    public void increment() {\\n        value++; // A non-atomic operation!\\n    }\\n\\n    public int getValue() {\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n}"],[1849,"Reactive programming is a paradigm oriented around working with streams of data and the automatic propagation of changes. Instead of requesting new data explicitly, you declare how the system should react when it appears. The key concepts are the Observable (an observable stream), the Observer and the operators that transform them. It is especially useful for handling events, HTTP requests or user input, where the data arrives asynchronously.","Reactive programming is a style of programming in which the main attention is given to streams of data and the automatic propagation of changes. Instead of an imperative approach, where you tell the computer how to do things step by step, you declaratively describe the relationships between the various sources of data (the streams) and how the application should react to them.\\n\\nThe key concepts\\n\\nThe main building blocks are:\\n\\n- The Observable: represents a stream of data or events that may be emitted over time. That can be a single value, many values or even an error.\\n\\n- The Observer: an object that subscribes to an Observable and reacts to the data arriving (next), to errors (error) or to the signal that the stream has finished (complete).\\n\\n- Operators: pure functions (such as map, filter, merge) that let you transform, combine and manipulate streams, creating new Observables.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nThis paradigm is ideally suited to scenarios with asynchronous, event-oriented data:\\n\\n- Handling user events (clicks, keyboard input).\\n\\n- Working with HTTP requests and WebSocket messages.\\n\\n- Managing state in complex front-end applications.\\n\\n- Processing data in real time, for example in dashboards or chats.\\n\\nA code example (RxJS)\\n\\nConsider a simple example of handling the user\'s input with a delay (debounce) for a search.\\n\\nIn this example we created a reactive stream from the input events. The `debounceTime` and `distinctUntilChanged` operators help optimise performance by reducing the number of useless search operations.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nReactive programming is worth applying when your application relies heavily on asynchronous events or streams of data. It makes the code more declarative and composable and simplifies the handling of complex scenarios, such as cancelling requests, merging streams or handling errors. For simple synchronous tasks, however, it can add unnecessary complexity.","import { fromEvent } from \'rxjs\';\\nimport { debounceTime, map, distinctUntilChanged } from \'rxjs/operators\';\\n\\n// Get a reference to the input field\\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById(\'search\');\\n\\n// Create an Observable from the input events\\nconst searchStream = fromEvent(searchInput, \'input\').pipe(\\n  // Turn the event into the field\'s value\\n  map(event => event.target.value),\\n  // Wait 300 ms after the last input\\n  debounceTime(300),\\n  // Ignore repeated values in a row\\n  distinctUntilChanged()\\n);\\n\\n// Subscribe to the stream\\nconst subscription = searchStream.subscribe(searchTerm => {\\n  console.log(\'Searching for:\', searchTerm);\\n  // An HTTP request could be sent here\\n});\\n\\n// You can unsubscribe later: subscription.unsubscribe();"],[1850,"A livelock is a state in multithreaded programming where two or more threads constantly change their state in response to each other\'s actions but cannot finish their work. Unlike a deadlock, the threads are not blocked but are actively running code; their interaction, however, leads to an endless cycle of mutual concessions. This often arises from unsuccessful conflict-resolution algorithms, for example when threads try to avoid a deadlock but yield resources to each other too \\"politely\\". The result is that the system stays alive but its performance drops, since no useful work is done.","A livelock is a specific concurrency problem in which processes or threads are actively running but make no progress on their task because of a cyclic dependency in their interaction. Unlike a deadlock, where threads simply wait having locked resources, in a livelock they carry on bustling about, consuming processor time but with no real progress.\\n\\nHow does a livelock arise?\\n\\nThe typical scenario is two threads trying to acquire two resources (A and B) but using a deadlock-avoidance algorithm: if a resource is taken, they release the one they have already acquired and retry later. If both threads synchronise their actions, they may acquire and release the resources simultaneously, never obtaining both at once.\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nIn this example both threads may print messages about acquiring and releasing forever but never finish, because their actions are synchronised — that is a livelock.\\n\\nWhere it is encountered and how to avoid it\\n\\n- Network protocols: for example, two nodes send packets at the same time, collide, back off and send again at the same moment.\\n\\n- Coordinating distributed systems: processes try to reach consensus but constantly change their decisions.\\n\\n- Ways of avoiding it: use random delays before retrying, thread priorities or more sophisticated algorithms (for example, with a backoff).\\n\\nConclusion: A livelock is an insidious problem, because the system seems to be working while in fact it is idling. It is important to design synchronisation algorithms so that threads do not fall into cyclic dependencies, and to add detection mechanisms (for example, a limit on the number of attempts).","import threading\\nimport time\\n\\nresource_a = threading.Lock()\\nresource_b = threading.Lock()\\n\\ndef worker1():\\n    while True:\\n        if resource_a.acquire(blocking=False):\\n            print(\\"Worker1 acquired A\\")\\n            time.sleep(0.1)  # Simulating work\\n            if resource_b.acquire(blocking=False):\\n                print(\\"Worker1 acquired B -> the work is finished\\")\\n                resource_b.release()\\n                resource_a.release()\\n                break\\n            else:\\n                print(\\"Worker1 cannot acquire B; it releases A\\")\\n                resource_a.release()\\n                time.sleep(0.1)  # A pause before retrying\\n\\ndef worker2():\\n    while True:\\n        if resource_b.acquire(blocking=False):\\n            print(\\"Worker2 acquired B\\")\\n            time.sleep(0.1)\\n            if resource_a.acquire(blocking=False):\\n                print(\\"Worker2 acquired A -> the work is finished\\")\\n                resource_a.release()\\n                resource_b.release()\\n                break\\n            else:\\n                print(\\"Worker2 cannot acquire A; it releases B\\")\\n                resource_b.release()\\n                time.sleep(0.1)\\n\\nthread1 = threading.Thread(target=worker1)\\nthread2 = threading.Thread(target=worker2)\\nthread1.start()\\nthread2.start()\\nthread1.join()\\nthread2.join()"],[1851,"A lock is a synchronisation primitive that lets only one thread execute a critical section of code at a time. It is used to protect shared data from being modified by several threads at once, which prevents a race condition. For example, in Python you can use threading.Lock(). A thread acquires the lock with the acquire() method, performs its operations and then releases it with release(). If the lock is already held by another thread, the current thread waits for it to be released.","A lock (often called a mutex) is the basic synchronisation mechanism in multithreaded programming. Its main purpose is to provide exclusive access to a shared resource (for example, a variable, a file, a data structure) for one thread at a particular moment in time. That prevents a race condition, where several threads read and change data at once, leading to unpredictable, erroneous results.\\n\\nHow a lock works\\n\\nA lock has two main states: locked and unlocked. When a thread wants to enter a critical section (a piece of code working with a shared resource), it tries to acquire the lock. If the lock is free, the thread acquires it and carries on executing. If the lock is already held by another thread, the current thread blocks (enters a waiting state) until the lock is released.\\n\\nAn example of use in Python\\n\\nConsider the classic example of incrementing a counter from several threads, without a lock and with one.\\n\\nWhere locks are used\\n\\n- Multithreaded applications: Protecting data structures (lists, dictionaries) in web servers and GUI applications.\\n\\n- Databases: Transactions often use locks to provide isolation (for example, SELECT FOR UPDATE).\\n\\n- Operating systems: Synchronising access to system resources (files, sockets).\\n\\n- Parallel algorithms: When several processes or threads have to coordinate access to shared state.\\n\\nImportant aspects and pitfalls\\n\\n- Deadlock: Can arise if two threads acquire several locks in different orders. The solution is always to acquire locks in the same order.\\n\\n- Starvation: If one thread holds a lock for a long time, the others may wait a long time.\\n\\n- Performance: Excessive use of locks can negate the advantages of multithreading.\\n\\n- Recursive locks (RLock): Let one thread acquire the same lock several times.\\n\\nConclusion: A lock is a fundamental tool for working safely with shared resources in a multithreaded environment. It is worth applying whenever several threads may change the same data at once. It is important to use locks carefully, however, so as to avoid deadlocks and not reduce performance.","import threading\\n\\n# The shared resource\\ncounter = 0\\n\\n# A function with no protection\\ndef increment_without_lock():\\n    global counter\\n    for _ in range(100000):\\n        counter += 1  # This is not an atomic operation!\\n\\n# A function protected with a Lock\\nlock = threading.Lock()\\ndef increment_with_lock():\\n    global counter\\n    for _ in range(100000):\\n        lock.acquire()  # Acquire the lock\\n        try:\\n            counter += 1\\n        finally:\\n            lock.release()  # Always release it\\n\\n# Create the threads\\nthreads = []\\nfor _ in range(10):\\n    t = threading.Thread(target=increment_with_lock)\\n    threads.append(t)\\n    t.start()\\n\\n# Wait for every thread to finish\\nfor t in threads:\\n    t.join()\\n\\nprint(f\\"Final counter: {counter}\\")  # It will be 1000000 only with the Lock"],[1852,"Conditional types let you choose a type depending on a condition. The syntax resembles the ternary operator: `T extends U ? X : Y`.","Conditional types let you compute a new type based on a logical condition. They work on this principle: if type `A` is compatible with type `B`, the result is one type, otherwise it is another. This is useful for generic data structures and functions whose types change depending on the input. Conditional types are most often used together with generics.\\n\\nIn the example below, the conditional type `IsString` returns `\'yes\'` when the given type is a string and `\'no\'` otherwise.","type IsString<T> = T extends string ? \'yes\' : \'no\';\\ntype A = IsString<string>; // \'yes\'\\ntype B = IsString<number>; // \'no\'"],[1853,"Mapped types let you transform every property of an existing type in a single pass. This is handy for deriving a type from another one with all fields made required, optional or read-only.","Mapped types take an existing type and apply a transformation to it through the `[P in keyof T]` syntax. This is how you create new types with changed modifiers (for example, adding `readonly` or `?` to every field). They are especially powerful in combination with `keyof` and conditional types — an object\'s structure can be changed dynamically without rewriting the types by hand.\\n\\nIn the example, the type `Readonly` makes every property of the given object read-only.","type Readonly<T> = {\\n  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P];\\n};\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\nconst user: Readonly<User> = {\\n  name: \'John\',\\n  age: 30,\\n};\\nuser.name = \'Doe\'; // error: property `name` is read-only"],[1854,"Template literal types construct new string types using template string syntax at the type level. They are useful for strictly typed routes, property names and other dynamic strings.","Template literal types combine string literals and other types to create new string types. The syntax mirrors ordinary template literals in JavaScript, but it works at the type level. This is convenient for type-safe string patterns: URI routes, field names, commands with dynamic parts.\\n\\nIn the example, the type `Route` describes the allowed routes that start with `/users/` followed by any string.","type Route = `/users/${string}`;\\n\\nlet validRoute: Route = \'/users/john\'; // correct\\nlet invalidRoute: Route = \'/posts/john\'; // error: the string does not match the pattern"],[1855,"These are conditional types that are automatically distributed over each member of a union type when the checked type is a naked generic parameter.","When a union is passed into the conditional type `T extends U ? X : Y` and `T` is a naked type parameter, TypeScript breaks the union apart into its individual members, applies the condition to each one and reassembles the result back into a union. This lets you transform complex types without duplicating code. Distributivity can be turned off by wrapping both sides in a tuple: `[T] extends [U] ? X : Y`.\\n\\nIn the example, the type `ToArray` creates a separate array for each type inside the union.","type ToArray<T> = T extends any ? T[] : never;\\ntype A = ToArray<number>; // number[]\\ntype B = ToArray<number | string>; // number[] | string[]"],[1856,"Utility types are built-in helper types (`Partial`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Required`, `Readonly`, `Record` and others) that transform existing types: extracting parts, changing modifiers, creating derived types.","Utility types are built into TypeScript and spare you from writing type transformations by hand. The most common ones are: `Partial<T>` (all fields optional), `Required<T>` (all required), `Readonly<T>` (read-only), `Pick<T, K>` and `Omit<T, K>` (select or exclude fields), `Record<K, T>` (an object with the given keys and value type), `ReturnType<F>`, `Parameters<F>`. They make code more compact and easier to maintain.\\n\\nIn the example, `Partial<User>` makes every property of the `User` interface optional.","interface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n  email?: string;\\n}\\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>; // every property becomes optional"],[1857,"Recursive types refer to themselves. They are needed to describe structures of arbitrary nesting depth — trees, linked lists, JSON.","A recursive type mentions its own name in its definition. This is useful for data of unknown nesting depth: a tree, a graph, JSON. For example, you can describe a JSON value where an object contains fields each of which may again be a JSON value. TypeScript supports recursion in type aliases and interfaces, but with restrictions on types that expand infinitely.\\n\\nIn the example, the type `JSONValue` describes any valid JSON value, including nested objects and arrays.","type JSONValue = string | number | boolean | JSONObject | JSONArray;\\ninterface JSONObject {\\n  [key: string]: JSONValue;\\n}\\ntype JSONArray = JSONValue[];\\nconst data: JSONValue = {\\n  name: \\"John\\",\\n  children: [\\n    {\\n      name: \\"Jane\\",\\n    },\\n  ],\\n};"],[1858,"A decorator is a special function that modifies a class, method, property or parameter. Decorators add metadata or change behaviour without touching the source code of the declaration.","Decorators are declared with `@` and applied to classes and their members; they run when the class is defined, not when it is called. The kinds are: a class decorator (modifies the constructor), a method decorator (changes the method\'s behaviour), a property decorator (validation, logging) and a parameter decorator (checking or transforming arguments). Typical uses are validation (`@MinLength(5)`), logging, caching and authorization, as well as the Angular and NestJS frameworks.\\n\\nIn the example, the `log` decorator wraps a method and logs the arguments of every call.","function log(target: any, key: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {\\n  const originalMethod = descriptor.value;\\n  descriptor.value = function (...args: any[]) {\\n    console.log(`Method ${key} was called with arguments: ${args}`);\\n    return originalMethod.apply(this, args);\\n  };\\n  return descriptor;\\n}\\nclass Calculator {\\n  @log\\n  add(a: number, b: number) {\\n    return a + b;\\n  }\\n}\\nconst calc = new Calculator();\\ncalc.add(2, 3); // In the console: \\"Method add was called with arguments: 2,3\\""],[1859,"Mixins let you reuse code across several classes by combining their functionality. They are an alternative to multiple inheritance, which TypeScript does not have.","A mixin is a function that takes a base class and returns a new class with added properties and methods. By applying several such functions in sequence you can assemble a class out of independent \\"pieces\\" of behaviour without duplication. The helper type `Constructor` is normally used to type the base class.\\n\\nIn the example, `Timestamped` is a mixin that adds a `timestamp` property to any class passed to it.","type Constructor<T = {}> = new (...args: any[]) => T;\\nfunction Timestamped<TBase extends Constructor>(Base: TBase) {\\n  return class extends Base {\\n    timestamp = Date.now();\\n  };\\n}\\nclass User {\\n  name: string;\\n  constructor(name: string) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n}\\nconst TimestampedUser = Timestamped(User);\\nconst user = new TimestampedUser(\\"Alice\\");\\nconsole.log(user.timestamp); // Prints the current time"],[1860,"An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly, only inherited from, and it may contain method implementations and fields. An interface is a contract without implementation; a class can implement several interfaces at once.","An abstract class is declared with the `abstract` keyword, may contain both abstract (bodyless) and implemented methods as well as fields and a constructor; you can inherit from only one such class. An interface describes only the signatures of properties and methods, exists only at compile time and does not appear in the compiled JavaScript; a class implements several interfaces through `implements`. The upshot: abstract classes suit shared template behaviour, interfaces suit describing a mandatory set of methods.","abstract class Animal {\\n  abstract makeSound(): void; // Must be implemented\\n  move() { console.log(\\"Moving...\\"); } // Already implemented\\n}\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  makeSound() { console.log(\\"Woof!\\"); }\\n}\\n\\ninterface Flyable {\\n  fly(): void;\\n}\\nclass Bird implements Flyable {\\n  fly() { console.log(\\"Flying!\\"); }\\n}"],[1861,"Describe the shape of objects with `type`/`interface`, use generics instead of `any`, narrow types with type guards, apply utility types for derived types and turn on strict mode (`strict: true` in `tsconfig.json`).","Reliable typing comes down to several practices: describe the shape of objects with `type` or `interface`; factor shared behaviour out into generics instead of `any`; use type guards (`typeof`, `instanceof`, `value is T` predicates) to narrow `unknown` safely; apply utility types (`Partial`, `Readonly`, `Pick`) for derived types; and turn on the compiler\'s strict checks. This reduces the number of runtime errors and makes the code easier to maintain.\\n\\nThe example shows a basic type alias, a generic function, a custom type guard and derived types built with utility types.","type User = {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  isAdmin?: boolean; // Optional field\\n};\\n\\nfunction getFirst<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {\\n  return arr[0];\\n}\\n\\nfunction isString(value: unknown): value is string {\\n  return typeof value === \'string\';\\n}\\n\\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>; // All fields optional\\ntype ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>; // Read-only"],[1862,"The `any` type lets a variable hold any value and lets you do anything with it, but it switches off type checking, which can lead to errors. The `unknown` type, by contrast, requires explicit type checks before use, which makes the code safer. Using unknown is recommended when the type of the data is not known in advance but type safety still has to be guaranteed.","In TypeScript the `any` type gives you complete freedom when working with a variable, but all type protection is lost in the process. For example, if a value is assigned to a variable of type any, TypeScript will not check whether that variable\'s methods can be called or its properties used, which can lead to errors at runtime:\\n\\nThe `unknown` type is a safer alternative. Like any, it lets you assign any value to a variable, but as soon as you try to interact with that variable TypeScript demands explicit type checks. This protects the program from unexpected behaviour. For example:\\n\\nSo unknown is recommended in situations where the type of a value is not known in advance (for example, when working with user input or API data). This type helps you write safer code, unlike any, which removes all checks and can lead to errors in unexpected places.","let data: any = \\"Hello\\";\\n// The error appears only at runtime, not at compile time\\nconsole.log(data.toFixed()); // TypeError: data.toFixed is not a function\\n\\nlet value: unknown = \\"Hello\\";\\n// TypeScript will not allow the method to be called without a type check\\nconsole.log(value.toFixed()); // Compile-time error\\nif (typeof value === \\"number\\") {\\n  console.log(value.toFixed()); // Now this is safe\\n}"],[1863,"Interfaces and types in TypeScript are very similar — both are used to describe the structure of objects — but there are differences. Interfaces suit describing objects and classes better and can extend (inherit from) one another. Types are more universal: they can combine primitive types, form unions or intersections of other types, but they cannot be extended the way interfaces can.","An interface and a type are two ways of declaring a data structure in TypeScript, and in most cases they can be used interchangeably. However, there are several key differences.\\n\\nInterfaces are made specifically for describing objects and classes. With them you can define which properties and methods an object must have. One advantage of interfaces is the ability to extend (inherit). One interface can inherit from another, adding new properties or methods to it:\\n\\nTypes are more flexible: they let you not only describe objects but also create unions or intersections of types and work with primitive values (strings, numbers and so on). For example, you can define that a variable may be either a string or a number:\\n\\nTypes, however, cannot extend one another the way interfaces do. Instead they can be combined with other types, which lets you build new types out of existing ones:\\n\\nTo sum up, interfaces are a better fit for describing complex data structures that need to be extended, especially in an object-oriented programming style. Types are more universal and can be used to describe any combination of data.","interface Person {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\ninterface Employee extends Person {\\n  position: string;\\n}\\nconst employee: Employee = {\\n  name: \\"Alice\\",\\n  age: 30,\\n  position: \\"Developer\\"\\n};\\n\\ntype StringOrNumber = string | number;\\nlet value: StringOrNumber = \\"Hello\\";\\nvalue = 42; // Also valid\\n\\ntype Animal = { species: string };\\ntype Dog = Animal & { breed: string };\\nconst myDog: Dog = {\\n  species: \\"Canine\\",\\n  breed: \\"Golden Retriever\\"\\n};"],[1864,"A type assertion in TypeScript lets you tell the compiler that a variable has a particular type, even when TypeScript cannot determine that automatically. It is used when the developer is sure about the type of the data despite TypeScript\'s inference. A type assertion can be written with the `as` syntax or with angle brackets (`<>`), but it should be applied with care, because it switches off some of the compiler\'s checks.","TypeScript uses a type inference system to determine the type of variables automatically from their value. In some cases, however, TypeScript may not have enough information to infer the correct type, and then it becomes necessary to state the type explicitly with a type assertion.\\n\\nFor example, when working with data received from an API, TypeScript may assign the type `unknown` or `any` if it does not know the exact shape of the data. In such situations a type assertion helps you specify the concrete data type, provided the developer knows what that type should be:\\n\\nThere are two syntaxes for a type assertion: using the as keyword or angle brackets (`<>`):\\n\\nIt is important to understand, however, that a type assertion does not change the actual value of the variable — it only tells the compiler how to treat it. If the type assertion is incorrect, it can lead to errors while the program is running:\\n\\nType assertions should be used with care. They are worth applying when you are sure TypeScript is wrong in its assumptions about a variable\'s type and you need to specify the correct type for the program to work properly.","let someValue: unknown = \\"This is a string\\";\\n\\n// We know this is a string, and we can state that with a type assertion\\nlet stringLength: number = (someValue as string).length;\\nconsole.log(stringLength); // 16\\n\\n// Using \\"as\\"\\nlet value: any = \\"Hello\\";\\nlet strLength: number = (value as string).length;\\n\\n// Using angle brackets\\nlet anotherStrLength: number = (<string>value).length;\\n\\nlet numberValue: any = 123;\\nconsole.log((numberValue as string).length); // Error at runtime, even though the compiler did not complain"],[1865,"An intersection type in TypeScript lets you combine several types into one, and the result includes all the properties of all the types. This is especially useful when you need to combine different data types or describe objects that must have several sets of properties at the same time. An intersection is created with the `&` operator and lets you build complex data structures flexibly.","Intersection types are a way of combining two or more types into one. Unlike union types, which let a variable be one of several types, an intersection requires the variable to satisfy all of the combined types at once.\\n\\nIntersection types are created with the & operator, and the resulting type includes every property defined in each type. This is useful when you need to create complex objects or functions that must satisfy several sets of requirements.\\n\\nConsider an example with an intersection type describing an object that must be both a person and an employee:\\n\\nHere the type `Worker` includes all the properties of the types `Person` and `Employee`. The object `worker` must contain both a name and an age (from `Person`) and an employee id and a position (from `Employee`).\\n\\nIt is important to understand that if the combined types contradict each other — for example, if the same property has different types — TypeScript will produce an error. Consider an example:\\n\\nIntersection types are useful when you need to describe complex objects that have to satisfy several requirements at once. This lets you create strictly typed data and avoid errors when using objects that contain many properties.","interface Person {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\ninterface Employee {\\n  employeeId: number;\\n  position: string;\\n}\\n// An intersection of the types Person and Employee\\ntype Worker = Person & Employee;\\nconst worker: Worker = {\\n  name: \\"John\\",\\n  age: 35,\\n  employeeId: 12345,\\n  position: \\"Developer\\"\\n};\\n\\ninterface Cat {\\n  name: string;\\n  purrs: boolean;\\n}\\ninterface Dog {\\n  name: string;\\n  barks: boolean;\\n  purrs: number; // A contradiction\\n}\\n// TypeScript will produce an error, because the types of the purrs property differ\\ntype Pet = Cat & Dog;"],[1866,"Union types let you state that a variable may hold values of different types. This is useful in situations where the data type can be flexible and change.","Union types in TypeScript let a variable have several possible types. This means a variable can be, for example, either a string or a number depending on the situation. It is convenient for handling different variants of data in a single function or variable. Such types are used with the `|` operator.\\n\\nHere the variable `value` can be either a string or a number, which lets you encode different usage scenarios.","let value: string | number;\\nvalue = \'Hello\'; // correct\\nvalue = 42; // also correct"],[1867,"Generics let you create components that work with different data types. This is useful for building more universal and flexible functions or classes.","Generics let you create functions, classes or interfaces that can work with various data types while keeping strict typing. This is especially useful for developing universal components. Instead of specifying a concrete type, a type variable is used, and it is supplied at the moment the function or class is used.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nIn this example the function `identity` works with any data type passed to it as an argument, which makes it very flexible.","function identity<T>(arg: T): T {\\n  return arg;\\n}\\nlet output1 = identity<string>(\'Hello\'); // T = string\\nlet output2 = identity<number>(42); // T = number"],[1868,"The `readonly` keyword lets you make a property of an object or a variable immutable. This means that once a value has been assigned, it cannot be changed.","In TypeScript the `readonly` keyword lets you declare properties of objects or arrays immutable after they have been initialized. This is useful when you need to protect data from accidental changes later on. Applying this approach improves data integrity and helps avoid errors.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nHere the property `name` is immutable, whereas `age` can be changed.","interface Person {\\n  readonly name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\nconst person: Person = { name: \'John\', age: 30 };\\nperson.age = 31; // correct\\nperson.name = \'Jack\'; // error: a readonly property cannot be changed"],[1869,"Type guards are constructs that let you check data types while the program is running. This matters when working with union types, where you need to know the exact data type at a particular moment.","Type guards help TypeScript determine the exact type of a variable while the program is running. They are especially useful when working with union types, where a variable may be one of several types. Type guards let you perform a type check with the `typeof` and `instanceof` operators, as well as with custom functions.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nThis example uses `typeof` to determine whether the variable is a string or a number and handle it accordingly.","function printValue(value: string | number) {\\n  if (typeof value === \'string\') {\\n    console.log(`String: ${value}`);\\n  } else {\\n    console.log(`Number: ${value}`);\\n  }\\n}"],[1870,"An enum is a special construct in TypeScript that lets you declare a set of named constants. This is useful for grouping related values such as statuses or operating modes.","An enum (enumeration) is a way of defining a set of constants to which numeric or string values can be assigned. It is useful when you need to work with a set of predefined values, for example order statuses or access levels. TypeScript supports both numeric and string enums.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nIn this example the enum `Direction` defines four directions, starting from the numeric value 1.","enum Direction {\\n  Up = 1,\\n  Down,\\n  Left,\\n  Right,\\n}\\nlet dir: Direction = Direction.Up;"],[1871,"The `strictNullChecks` flag in TypeScript makes checking for `null` and `undefined` mandatory, preventing the implicit use of those values in your code. This helps avoid the typical errors associated with undefined values.","When the `strictNullChecks` flag is enabled, TypeScript does not allow variables to hold `null` or `undefined` by default unless those are stated explicitly in the type. This means that all checks for the presence of those values become mandatory, which helps avoid errors where functions or variables unexpectedly turn out to be undefined. This mode makes code safer.\\n\\nExample:","let name: string | null = null; // correct\\nname = \'John\'; // correct\\nname = undefined; // error, unless stated in the type"],[1872,"Modules in TypeScript let you split code into independent parts and reuse it. This helps structure a project and avoid name conflicts.","Modules are the foundation for organizing code in large projects. They let you import and export functions, classes and variables between files, which makes code easier to maintain and scale. TypeScript uses two kinds of modules: ES modules (`import`/`export`) and CommonJS modules. ES modules are supported by most modern browsers and runtimes.\\n\\nExample:","// user.ts\\nexport class User {\\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\\n}\\n// main.ts\\nimport { User } from \'./user\';\\nconst user = new User(\'John\');"],[1873,"TypeScript adds static typing, which improves code quality and reduces errors. It does, however, require compilation and has a more complex setup. It suits large projects and team development.","TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript with static typing.\\n\\nPros:\\n- Static typing: catching errors at compile time\\n- Autocompletion: better IDE support\\n- Refactoring: safe code changes\\n- Documentation: types as code documentation\\n- Compatibility: full compatibility with JavaScript\\n\\nCons:\\n- Compilation: requires an extra build step\\n- Learning curve: additional concepts to learn\\n- Setup: more complex project configuration\\n- Bundle size: larger code size after compilation\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\n- For large and complex projects\\n- In development teams\\n- When code reliability and maintainability matter","// A TypeScript example with types\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n}\\n\\nfunction createUser(user: User): User {\\n  return {\\n    id: user.id,\\n    name: user.name,\\n    email: user.email\\n  };\\n}"],[1874,"TypeScript must be compiled to JavaScript in order to run. ts-node lets you run TypeScript code without compiling it first, which is convenient for development but not for production.","Compiling TypeScript is necessary because Node.js and browsers execute JavaScript, not TypeScript.\\n\\nThe compilation process:\\n- Writing TypeScript code (`.ts` files)\\n- Compiling with `tsc` (the TypeScript Compiler)\\n- Getting JavaScript code (`.js` files)\\n- Executing the JavaScript code\\n\\nts-node is a utility that:\\n- Compiles TypeScript in memory\\n- Executes the resulting JavaScript code\\n- Does not create `.js` files on disk\\n- Is convenient for development and testing\\n\\nDifferences from production:\\n- Development: use ts-node for a quick start\\n- Production: compile to JavaScript and run the `.js` files","# Installing ts-node\\nnpm install -g ts-node\\n\\n# Running a TypeScript file\\nts-node index.ts"],[1875,"`Boolean(-1)` will return `true`, because any non-zero number converts to `true`. `Boolean(~-1)` will return `false`, because `~-1` equals `0`, and zero converts to `false`.","Conversion to Boolean in JavaScript follows the rules of \\"truthy\\" and \\"falsy\\" values.\\n\\nThe rules for converting numbers:\\n- `0` → `false`\\n- Any other number (including negative ones) → `true`\\n- `NaN` → `false`\\n\\nThe bitwise NOT operation (`~`):\\n- Inverts the bits of a number\\n- `~x` is equivalent to `-(x + 1)`\\n\\nThe calculations:\\n\\nThe full example:","// Boolean(-1)\\n-1 !== 0 // true\\nBoolean(-1) // true\\n\\n// Computing ~-1\\n~-1 = -(-1 + 1) = -(0) = 0\\n\\n// Boolean(0)\\n0 === 0 // true\\nBoolean(0) // false\\n\\nconsole.log(Boolean(-1));   // true\\nconsole.log(Boolean(~-1));  // false\\nconsole.log(~-1);           // 0"],[1876,"Utility types are convenient because they save time, reduce the amount of code and provide type safety without duplication. They let you create derived types from existing ones, keeping them automatically in sync when things change. This makes code more maintainable and less error-prone.","Utility types in TypeScript provide a powerful way of manipulating types without creating redundant interfaces.\\n\\nThe advantages of utility types:\\n\\n1. DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself):\\n\\n2. Automatic synchronization:\\n\\n3. Flexibility and composition:\\n\\nPopular utility types:\\n\\nPartial — all properties optional:\\n\\nRequired — all properties required:\\n\\nPick — selecting specific properties:\\n\\nOmit — excluding properties:\\n\\nReadonly — read-only properties:\\n\\nA practical example:","// Instead of this:\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n  createdAt: Date;\\n}\\n\\ninterface UserUpdate {\\n  id?: number;\\n  name?: string;\\n  email?: string;\\n  createdAt?: Date;\\n}\\n\\n// Use this:\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n  createdAt: Date;\\n}\\n\\ntype UserUpdate = Partial<User>;\\n\\ninterface Product {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  price: number;\\n  category: string;\\n}\\n\\n// When Product changes, ProductPreview updates automatically\\ntype ProductPreview = Pick<Product, \'id\' | \'name\' | \'price\'>;\\n\\n// An example of use\\nfunction displayProduct(preview: ProductPreview) {\\n  console.log(`${preview.name}: $${preview.price}`);\\n}\\n\\n// Combining utility types\\ntype ApiResponse<T> = {\\n  data: T;\\n  status: number;\\n  message: string;\\n};\\n\\ntype UserApiResponse = ApiResponse<User>;\\ntype ProductApiResponse = ApiResponse<Product>;\\n\\n// A more complex composition\\ntype Editable<T> = Partial<T> & { id: number };\\ntype UserEditable = Editable<User>;\\n\\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>;\\n// { id?: number; name?: string; ... }\\n\\ntype RequiredUser = Required<PartialUser>;\\n// { id: number; name: string; ... }\\n\\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, \'id\' | \'name\'>;\\n// { id: number; name: string }\\n\\ntype UserWithoutId = Omit<User, \'id\'>;\\n// { name: string; email: string; ... }\\n\\ntype ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;\\n// { readonly id: number; readonly name: string; ... }\\n\\n// Forms often need partial data\\ntype UserForm = Partial<Pick<User, \'name\' | \'email\'>>;\\n\\n// For API responses — a specific set of fields\\ntype UserApi = Pick<User, \'id\' | \'name\'> & {\\n  profileUrl: string;\\n};\\n\\n// For internal logic — excluding sensitive data\\ntype SafeUser = Omit<User, \'password\' | \'token\'>;"],[1877,"`typeof` in TypeScript (in a type context) is used to obtain the type of a variable or constant. `keyof` is used to obtain a type representing all the keys (property names) of another type. The combination `keyof typeof` first obtains the type of a value with `typeof` and then extracts the type of its keys with `keyof`. This lets you create a union type out of the property names of an object defined as a constant.","These operators are the foundation for building dynamic, strictly typed constructs in TypeScript.\\n\\ntypeof (Type Query Operator)\\nIn TypeScript `typeof` can be used in two contexts:\\n- In a value position: as in JavaScript, it returns a string with the name of the variable\'s type at runtime.\\n- In a type position: it extracts the type of a variable declared with `const`, `let` or `function`.\\n\\n`keyof` (Index Type Query Operator)\\n`keyof` takes an object type and produces a union type of its keys.\\n\\nThe keyof typeof combination\\nThis combination is extremely useful when you have a constant object and want to create a type out of its keys.\\n\\nConclusion: the `keyof` and `typeof` operators (and especially their combination) are powerful tools for deriving types from existing JavaScript code, which lets you write safer, more maintainable TypeScript while avoiding duplication.","const person = { name: \'Alice\', age: 30 };\\n\\n// `typeof person` in a type context extracts the object type { name: string; age: number; }\\ntype PersonType = typeof person;\\n// Equivalent to: type PersonType = { name: string; age: number; }\\n\\ntype Person = { name: string; age: number };\\ntype PersonKeys = keyof Person; // Result: \\"name\\" | \\"age\\"\\n\\n// Suppose we have a configuration object\\nconst AppConfig = {\\n  theme: \'dark\',\\n  language: \'en\',\\n  version: \'1.0.0\'\\n} as const; // `as const` makes the properties readonly literals\\n\\n// 1. `typeof AppConfig` gives the type: { readonly theme: \\"dark\\"; readonly language: \\"en\\"; readonly version: \\"1.0.0\\"; }\\n// 2. `keyof typeof AppConfig` takes the keys of that type.\\ntype ConfigKey = keyof typeof AppConfig; // Result: \\"theme\\" | \\"language\\" | \\"version\\"\\n\\n// Now we can use this type for a function\\nfunction getConfig(key: ConfigKey) {\\n  return AppConfig[key];\\n}\\n\\ngetConfig(\'theme\'); // OK\\ngetConfig(\'invalidKey\'); // TS error: the argument is not assignable to the type \\"theme\\" | \\"language\\" | \\"version\\""],[1878,"Mapped types are useful when you need to create a new type from an old one by applying the same transformation to every property of the source type. They are a perfect fit for tasks that require making all properties optional (`Partial`) or read-only (`Readonly`), or selecting a subset of properties by name. They are a powerful tool for avoiding routine code duplication when defining similar types.","Mapped types let you iterate over the keys of an existing type and build a new type from them.\\n\\nSyntax and basic examples:\\nThe syntax is `{ [P in K]: T }`, where `P` is each key from the union type `K` and `T` is the type the property is transformed into.\\n- Creating utility types: TypeScript\'s standard utilities are implemented with mapped types.\\n\\n- Changing property modifiers: you can remove or add the `readonly` and `?` modifiers at the same time.\\n\\n- Changing property types: you can do more than copy — you can transform the type of every property.\\n\\nConclusion: mapped types are a tool for metaprogramming inside TypeScript\'s type system. They are indispensable for creating families of related types, ensuring consistency and reducing boilerplate, especially when working with state, configuration and DTOs (Data Transfer Objects).","// Make all properties optional\\ntype MyPartial<T> = {\\n  [P in keyof T]?: T[P];\\n};\\n\\n// Make all properties read-only\\ntype MyReadonly<T> = {\\n  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P];\\n};\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\ntype PartialUser = MyPartial<User>; // { name?: string; age?: number; }\\ntype ReadonlyUser = MyReadonly<User>; // { readonly name: string; readonly age: number; }\\n\\n// Remove optionality and readonly from all properties\\ntype Concrete<T> = {\\n  -readonly [P in keyof T]-?: T[P];\\n};\\n\\n// Create a type where every property is a function returning void\\ntype EventHandlers<T> = {\\n  [P in keyof T as `on${Capitalize<string & P>}`]: () => void;\\n};\\n// For the type { name: string; age: number } we get:\\n// { onName: () => void; onAge: () => void; }"],[1879,"Structural typing is an approach in which type compatibility is determined by structure (fields and methods) rather than by explicit declarations or names. TypeScript checks that an object has all the required properties with the right types, regardless of which type was declared for that object.","Structural typing in TypeScript\\nStructural typing is a foundational concept of TypeScript\'s type system, and it differs from the nominal typing used in languages such as Java or C#.\\n\\nThe core concept\\nUnder structural typing, two types are considered compatible if they have the same structure:\\n\\nComparison with nominal typing\\nStructural typing:\\n- Checks the shape of objects\\n- Types with the same structure are compatible\\n- Used in TypeScript, Go\\n\\nNominal typing:\\n- Checks explicit type declarations\\n- Requires explicit inheritance or implementation\\n- Used in Java, C#\\n\\nExamples of structural typing\\n\\n1. Object compatibility\\n\\n2. Function compatibility\\n\\nThe advantages of structural typing\\n\\n- Flexibility: does not require explicit type declarations\\n- Decomposition: lets you combine types easily\\n- Practicality: the focus is on what an object can do, not what it is\\n\\nPotential problems\\n\\nUse in practice\\nStructural typing is useful when working with external libraries:\\n\\nConclusion: structural typing makes TypeScript a flexible and practical language, letting you work with objects based on their actual structure rather than on formal type declarations.","interface Person {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\n// This object is compatible with Person, even though it is not declared as Person\\nconst user = { name: \\"Ivan\\", age: 30 };\\nconst person: Person = user; // OK - the structure matches\\n\\ninterface Point {\\n  x: number;\\n  y: number;\\n}\\n\\n// An object with extra properties is compatible too\\nconst point3D = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };\\nconst point: Point = point3D; // OK\\n\\nfunction printPoint(p: Point) {\\n  console.log(p.x, p.y);\\n}\\n\\nprintPoint(point3D); // OK - the Point structure is present\\n\\ntype Adder = (a: number, b: number) => number;\\n\\n// A function is compatible if its parameters and return value are compatible\\nconst multiply: Adder = (x: number, y: number) => x * y; // OK\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\n// Both objects are structurally compatible with User\\nconst correctUser = { id: 1, name: \\"Ivan\\" };\\nconst wrongUser = { id: \\"1\\", name: \\"Ivan\\" }; // Error - id must be a number\\n\\n// But this problem is caught at compile time\\n\\n// We can use objects that structurally match\\n// the expected types without an explicit type cast\\nfetch(\'/api/users\')\\n  .then(res => res.json())\\n  .then((users: User[]) => {\\n    // TypeScript will check the structure of the data received\\n  });"],[1880,"The object will be accepted without errors, because TypeScript uses structural typing. The compiler checks only the object\'s structure (the presence of the needed fields with the right types), not the type\'s name. If the structures match, the types are considered compatible.","Compatibility of types with the same structure\\nIn TypeScript type names play no part in compatibility checking — only the structure matters.\\n\\nA basic example\\n\\nTypes with an identical structure\\nEven when the types are declared completely separately:\\n\\nDifferent types with overlapping structure\\n\\nPractical scenarios\\n\\n1. Working with an API\\n\\n2. Functions with parameters\\n\\nRestrictions on direct assignment\\nTypeScript is stricter when assigning object literals directly:\\n\\nConclusion: TypeScript ignores type names entirely when checking compatibility. Only the structure matters — the presence of all required fields with compatible types. This makes the type system flexible and practical.","interface Person {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\nclass Employee {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n  salary: number;\\n}\\n\\n// Employee is compatible with Person, because it contains all the needed fields\\nconst person: Person = new Employee(); // OK\\n\\nfunction greet(p: Person) {\\n  console.log(`Hello, ${p.name}`);\\n}\\n\\ngreet(new Employee()); // OK - the structure matches\\n\\ninterface Car {\\n  brand: string;\\n  year: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Vehicle {\\n  brand: string;\\n  year: number;\\n}\\n\\nconst car: Car = { brand: \\"Toyota\\", year: 2020 };\\nconst vehicle: Vehicle = car; // OK - the structures are identical\\n\\nfunction processCar(c: Car) { /* ... */ }\\nprocessCar(vehicle); // OK\\n\\ninterface Animal {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Pet {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n  owner: string;\\n}\\n\\nconst animal: Animal = { name: \\"Barsik\\", age: 2 };\\nconst pet: Pet = { name: \\"Sharik\\", age: 3, owner: \\"Ivan\\" };\\n\\n// Pet is compatible with Animal (it has all the needed fields)\\nconst animalFromPet: Animal = pet; // OK\\n\\n// Animal is not compatible with Pet (the owner field is missing)\\n// const petFromAnimal: Pet = animal; // Error!\\n\\n// A type from our own system\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n}\\n\\n// Data from an API may have a different structure but still be compatible\\nconst apiResponse = {\\n  id: 1,\\n  name: \\"Ivan\\",\\n  email: \\"ivan@example.com\\",\\n  createdAt: \\"2023-01-01\\"\\n};\\n\\nconst user: User = apiResponse; // OK - all the required fields are there\\n\\ninterface Config {\\n  timeout: number;\\n  retries: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction setupAPI(config: Config) {\\n  // ...\\n}\\n\\n// Passing an object with extra properties\\nsetupAPI({\\n  timeout: 5000,\\n  retries: 3,\\n  baseURL: \\"/api\\" // An extra property - OK\\n});\\n\\ninterface Point {\\n  x: number;\\n  y: number;\\n}\\n\\n// Excess properties are not allowed on direct assignment\\n// const p: Point = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 }; // Error!\\n\\n// But through a variable - OK\\nconst point3D = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };\\nconst p: Point = point3D; // OK"],[1881,"Generics are used to create reusable components that work with different data types while preserving type safety. They let you parameterize types so that the same code can work with various types without losing type information at compile time.","Using generics in TypeScript\\nGenerics provide a way of creating components that can work with various types rather than with one concrete type.\\n\\nThe core problem without generics\\nWithout generics we are forced to use `any`, which means losing type safety:\\n\\nThe solution with generics\\n\\nThe main usage scenarios\\n\\n1. Universal functions\\n\\n2. Universal interfaces\\n\\n3. Universal classes\\n\\nGeneric constraints\\nYou can constrain the types a generic is allowed to work with:\\n\\nAdvanced scenarios\\n\\n1. Default generics\\n\\n2. Several type parameters\\n\\nConclusion: generics are essential for creating reusable, type-safe code. They let you write flexible components that preserve type information and provide compile-time checking.","// The problem: the type information is lost\\nfunction identity(value: any): any {\\n  return value;\\n}\\n\\nconst result = identity(42); // The type of result is any, not number\\n\\n// The solution: the type is preserved\\nfunction identity<T>(value: T): T {\\n  return value;\\n}\\n\\nconst num = identity(42);        // Type: number\\nconst str = identity(\\"hello\\");   // Type: string\\nconst bool = identity(true);     // Type: boolean\\n\\nfunction getFirst<T>(array: T[]): T | undefined {\\n  return array[0];\\n}\\n\\nconst numbers = getFirst([1, 2, 3]);     // Type: number | undefined\\nconst strings = getFirst([\\"a\\", \\"b\\", \\"c\\"]); // Type: string | undefined\\n\\ninterface Response<T> {\\n  data: T;\\n  status: number;\\n  message: string;\\n}\\n\\nconst userResponse: Response<User> = {\\n  data: { id: 1, name: \\"Ivan\\" },\\n  status: 200,\\n  message: \\"OK\\"\\n};\\n\\nconst productResponse: Response<Product> = {\\n  data: { id: 1, title: \\"Phone\\", price: 1000 },\\n  status: 200,\\n  message: \\"OK\\"\\n};\\n\\nclass Stack<T> {\\n  private items: T[] = [];\\n\\n  push(item: T): void {\\n    this.items.push(item);\\n  }\\n\\n  pop(): T | undefined {\\n    return this.items.pop();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst numberStack = new Stack<number>();\\nnumberStack.push(1);\\nnumberStack.push(2);\\nconst num = numberStack.pop(); // Type: number | undefined\\n\\nconst stringStack = new Stack<string>();\\nstringStack.push(\\"hello\\");\\n\\ninterface HasLength {\\n  length: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction logLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): void {\\n  console.log(item.length);\\n}\\n\\nlogLength(\\"a string\\");  // OK - a string has length\\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]);   // OK - an array has length\\n// logLength(42);       // Error - a number has no length\\n\\ninterface Pagination<T = any> {\\n  data: T[];\\n  page: number;\\n  total: number;\\n}\\n\\n// You can state the type explicitly or fall back to any\\nconst usersPagination: Pagination<User> = { /* ... */ };\\nconst defaultPagination: Pagination = { /* ... */ }; // T = any\\n\\nfunction pair<K, V>(key: K, value: V): [K, V] {\\n  return [key, value];\\n}\\n\\nconst stringNumber = pair(\\"age\\", 30);    // Type: [string, number]\\nconst numberBoolean = pair(1, true);     // Type: [number, boolean]"],[1882,"`any` represents any possible type and switches off type checking, while `never` represents the type of values that never occur. `any` is a supertype of all types, whereas `never` is a subtype of all types. `any` means \\"any type\\", while `never` means \\"no type at all\\".","The difference between any and never\\nThese two types represent opposite concepts in TypeScript\'s type system.\\n\\nThe any type\\n`any` switches off type checking completely:\\n\\nWhen to use any:\\n\\n- When migrating from JavaScript\\n- When working with dynamic data\\n- Temporarily, when the type is unknown\\n\\nThe never type\\n`never` represents values that never occur:\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n1. Type compatibility\\n\\n2. In unions and intersections\\n\\nPractical use\\n\\n1. Guarding against unhandled cases\\n\\n2. Filtering types\\n\\n3. Error handling\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nUse any:\\n- For a gradual migration from JavaScript\\n- When working with third-party libraries that have no types\\n- Temporarily, when a type is hard to express\\n\\nUse never:\\n- For functions that always throw\\n- For exhaustiveness checks\\n- In conditional types, for filtering\\n\\nConclusion: `any` switches off the type system and should be used as little as possible, whereas `never` strengthens type safety by describing impossible states and guaranteeing that checks are exhaustive.","let variable: any = \\"a string\\";\\nvariable = 42;          // OK\\nvariable = true;        // OK\\nvariable = [1, 2, 3];   // OK\\n\\n// No type checks at all\\nvariable.nonExistentMethod(); // Fails only at runtime\\n\\n// A function that always throws\\nfunction throwError(message: string): never {\\n  throw new Error(message);\\n}\\n\\n// A function with an infinite loop\\nfunction infiniteLoop(): never {\\n  while (true) {\\n    // ...\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An exhaustiveness check\\ntype Shape = \\"circle\\" | \\"square\\";\\n\\nfunction getArea(shape: Shape): number {\\n  switch (shape) {\\n    case \\"circle\\": return Math.PI;\\n    case \\"square\\": return 1;\\n    default: \\n      const exhaustiveCheck: never = shape;\\n      return exhaustiveCheck;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// any is compatible with any type\\nlet a: any;\\nlet b: number = a;      // OK\\nlet c: string = a;      // OK\\n\\n// never is compatible only with never\\nlet n: never;\\n// let x: number = n;   // Error!\\n// let y: string = n;   // Error!\\nlet z: never = n;       // OK\\n\\n// In unions\\ntype T1 = string | any;     // any\\ntype T2 = string | never;   // string\\n\\n// In intersections  \\ntype T3 = string & any;     // any\\ntype T4 = string & never;   // never\\n\\ntype Action = \\"CREATE\\" | \\"UPDATE\\" | \\"DELETE\\";\\n\\nfunction handleAction(action: Action) {\\n  switch (action) {\\n    case \\"CREATE\\": /* ... */ break;\\n    case \\"UPDATE\\": /* ... */ break;\\n    // case \\"DELETE\\": we forgot to handle this one\\n    default:\\n      const unexpected: never = action;\\n      throw new Error(`Unhandled action: ${unexpected}`);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Removes null and undefined from a type\\ntype NonNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;\\n\\ntype A = NonNullable<string | null>;        // string\\ntype B = NonNullable<number | undefined>;   // number\\ntype C = NonNullable<boolean | null | undefined>; // boolean\\n\\nfunction processData(data: string): string | never {\\n  if (!data) {\\n    throw new Error(\\"The data is required\\");\\n  }\\n  return data.toUpperCase();\\n}\\n\\nconst result = processData(\\"\\"); // Throws an error - the type is never"],[1883,"Yes, you can create your own error type. To do so you create a class that inherits from the built-in `Error` class and add the properties and methods you need. In the constructor you must call `super()` and set `Object.setPrototypeOf(this, CustomError.prototype)` so that `instanceof` works correctly.","Creating custom error types\\nYour own error types let you create specialized exceptions that carry additional information.\\n\\nA basic custom error class\\n\\nSpecialized errors with additional properties\\n\\nUsing custom errors\\n\\nHandling custom errors\\n\\nAn error hierarchy\\nYou can build complex error hierarchies:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n1. Always set the prototype\\n\\n2. Use the right name\\n\\n3. Add an error code\\n\\nConclusion: creating custom error types lets you structure exception handling, add contextual information and build a clear error hierarchy for better error management in your application.","class CustomError extends Error {\\n  constructor(message: string) {\\n    super(message);\\n    \\n    // Important for instanceof to work correctly\\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, CustomError.prototype);\\n    \\n    // Preserve the call stack\\n    if (Error.captureStackTrace) {\\n      Error.captureStackTrace(this, CustomError);\\n    }\\n    \\n    this.name = \\"CustomError\\";\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass ValidationError extends Error {\\n  public readonly field: string;\\n  public readonly value: any;\\n\\n  constructor(field: string, value: any, message?: string) {\\n    super(message || `Validation error on the field ${field}`);\\n    \\n    this.name = \\"ValidationError\\";\\n    this.field = field;\\n    this.value = value;\\n    \\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, ValidationError.prototype);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass NetworkError extends Error {\\n  public readonly statusCode: number;\\n  public readonly url: string;\\n\\n  constructor(statusCode: number, url: string, message?: string) {\\n    super(message || `Network error ${statusCode} for ${url}`);\\n    \\n    this.name = \\"NetworkError\\";\\n    this.statusCode = statusCode;\\n    this.url = url;\\n    \\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, NetworkError.prototype);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction validateUser(user: any): void {\\n  if (!user.name) {\\n    throw new ValidationError(\\"name\\", user.name, \\"The name is required\\");\\n  }\\n  \\n  if (user.age < 0) {\\n    throw new ValidationError(\\"age\\", user.age, \\"The age cannot be negative\\");\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {\\n  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`)\\n    .then(response => {\\n      if (!response.ok) {\\n        throw new NetworkError(response.status, `/api/users/${id}`);\\n      }\\n      return response.json();\\n    });\\n}\\n\\ntry {\\n  validateUser({ age: -1 });\\n} catch (error) {\\n  if (error instanceof ValidationError) {\\n    console.error(`Error on the field ${error.field}:`, error.message);\\n    // Error on the field age: The age cannot be negative\\n  } else {\\n    console.error(\\"Unknown error:\\", error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nabstract class AppError extends Error {\\n  public abstract readonly code: string;\\n  \\n  constructor(message: string) {\\n    super(message);\\n    this.name = this.constructor.name;\\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, new.target.prototype);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass DatabaseError extends AppError {\\n  public readonly code = \\"DB_ERROR\\";\\n  public readonly query: string;\\n\\n  constructor(query: string, message: string) {\\n    super(message);\\n    this.query = query;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass UniqueConstraintError extends DatabaseError {\\n  public readonly code = \\"UNIQUE_CONSTRAINT\\";\\n  public readonly constraint: string;\\n\\n  constructor(constraint: string, query: string) {\\n    super(query, `Unique constraint violated: ${constraint}`);\\n    this.constraint = constraint;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Without this instanceof will not work correctly\\nObject.setPrototypeOf(this, CustomError.prototype);\\n\\nthis.name = this.constructor.name; // Sets the name automatically\\n\\nclass BusinessError extends Error {\\n  public readonly code: string;\\n  \\n  constructor(code: string, message: string) {\\n    super(message);\\n    this.code = code;\\n    this.name = \\"BusinessError\\";\\n    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, BusinessError.prototype);\\n  }\\n}"],[1884,"TypeScript cannot run directly in the browser, because browsers understand only JavaScript. TypeScript is compiled (transformed) into ordinary JavaScript by a special tool — the TypeScript compiler. This process removes all TypeScript types and constructs, leaving pure JavaScript code. The resulting JavaScript can then be executed in any browser, just like ordinary JavaScript code.","TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing and other features, but browsers can only execute JavaScript code. That is why TypeScript has to be transformed into JavaScript before it is used in the browser.\\n\\nHow TypeScript works in the browser\\n\\n1. Compiling TypeScript to JavaScript\\nTypeScript code goes through the TypeScript compiler (tsc), which:\\n- Checks types for errors\\n- Removes type annotations and TypeScript-specific constructs\\n- Generates equivalent JavaScript code\\n\\nAn example of TypeScript code:\\n\\nAfter compilation we get JavaScript:\\n\\n2. Compilation tools\\nThe TypeScript Compiler (tsc)\\n- The official compiler from Microsoft\\n- Can be run from the command line or as part of a bundler\\n\\nBabel with TypeScript plugins\\n- An alternative way of transpiling\\n- Often used in modern builds\\n\\n3. Configuring compilation\\nThe `tsconfig.json` file controls the compilation process:\\n\\n4. Integration into the development process\\nDevelopment mode:\\n- Hot reload with automatic recompilation\\n- Source maps for debugging\\n\\nProduction mode:\\n- Minification and optimization\\n- Removal of comments and unnecessary whitespace\\n\\nA practical workflow example\\n\\nAfter compilation into `app.js`:\\n\\nKey aspects\\n\\nThe advantages of compilation\\n\\n- Catching errors at compile time — typical errors are found before the code runs in the browser\\n- Compatibility with older browsers — compiling down to older ES versions\\n- Optimization — removing unused code and types\\n\\nLimitations\\n\\n- Compilation time — an extra step in the development process\\n- Build size — types are removed, but the compiler may add polyfills\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript should be used when you need static typing and early error detection, but it is important to remember that the browser only receives JavaScript code after compilation. For small projects, or when maximum client-side performance matters, using plain JavaScript may be worth considering.","function greet(name: string): string {\\n    return `Hello, ${name}!`;\\n}\\n\\nfunction greet(name) {\\n    return `Hello, ${name}!`;\\n}\\n\\n{\\n  \\"compilerOptions\\": {\\n    \\"target\\": \\"ES5\\",\\n    \\"module\\": \\"ES6\\",\\n    \\"outDir\\": \\"./dist\\",\\n    \\"strict\\": true\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The original TypeScript (app.ts)\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\nfunction getUserInfo(user: User): string {\\n  return `User ${user.name} has ID: ${user.id}`;\\n}\\n\\nfunction getUserInfo(user) {\\n  return \\"User \\" + user.name + \\" has ID: \\" + user.id;\\n}"],[1885,"TypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript with support for static typing. It lets you find errors while you are still developing. TypeScript improves the readability and maintainability of code. It is especially useful in large projects. TypeScript code is compiled to ordinary JavaScript.","TypeScript is a programming language that extends JavaScript with a type system.\\n\\nWhy TypeScript is needed\\nTypeScript solves several key problems:\\n- it reduces the number of runtime errors\\n- it makes code more predictable\\n- it improves autocompletion and navigation in the IDE\\n\\nHow TypeScript is used in frontend work\\nIn frontend development TypeScript:\\n- describes component props\\n- types state and API responses\\n- makes refactoring safer\\n\\nAn example of simple typing:\\n\\nWhen TypeScript is especially useful\\n\\n- large teams\\n- long-lived projects\\n- complex business logic\\n- active refactoring\\n\\nLimitations\\n\\n- it takes time to learn\\n- it increases the initial setup\\n- types do not exist at runtime\\n\\nConclusion: TypeScript increases the reliability and scalability of frontend code, especially in large projects.","type User = {\\n  id: number\\n  name: string\\n}"],[1886,"`interface` is more convenient for describing objects and public contracts. `type` is more universal and suits unions, primitives and complex types. `interface` is easy to extend, `type` is easy to combine. In most cases the choice depends on the task and the project\'s style. The two tools are often used together.","`interface` and `type` solve similar problems but have different strengths.\\n\\nWhen interface is more convenient\\n`interface` is a better fit:\\n- for describing objects\\n- for public APIs\\n- for component contracts\\n\\nIts characteristics:\\n- it supports extension\\n- it reads well\\n- it is often used for React props\\n\\nWhen type is more convenient\\n`type` is more universal and is used:\\n- for union types\\n- for primitives\\n- for complex combinations\\n\\nThe key practical difference\\n\\n- `interface` — for structure\\n- `type` — for composition\\n\\nHow it is usually done in practice\\nIn many projects:\\n- `interface` is used for objects\\n- `type` — for everything else\\n\\nConclusion: `interface` and `type` do not compete but complement each other — it is important to understand what each is for and to use them deliberately.","interface User {\\n  id: number\\n  name: string\\n}\\n\\ntype Status = \'loading\' | \'success\' | \'error\'"],[1887,"`interface` can be extended with `extends` and with declaration merging. `type` is extended through intersections (`&`). `interface` is a better fit for public contracts and APIs. `type` is more flexible but does not support automatic merging of declarations. These differences matter when designing types.","In TypeScript both `interface` and `type` can be extended, but the mechanisms and the consequences differ.\\n\\nExtending an interface\\n`interface` supports:\\n- `extends`\\n- declaration merging\\n\\nAs a result `User` will contain both fields.\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n- contracts are convenient to extend\\n- it suits libraries\\n- it scales well\\n\\nExtending a type\\n`type` is extended through intersection types:\\n\\nIts characteristics:\\n- there is no declaration merging\\n- the composition is more explicit\\n- it is better for complex types\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- `interface` can be declared several times\\n- `type` cannot\\n- `type` supports unions (`|`)\\n- `interface` does not\\n\\nThe practical choice\\n\\n- public contracts → `interface`\\n- type composition → `type`\\n\\nConclusion: `interface` is better for extensible contracts, `type` for complex type logic and composition.","interface User {\\n  id: number\\n}\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string\\n}\\n\\ntype User = {\\n  id: number\\n}\\n\\ntype ExtendedUser = User & {\\n  name: string\\n}"],[1888,"Type predicates are functions that help TypeScript refine the type of a value. They return a `boolean` but have a special signature. Such functions are used in conditions. After the check TypeScript \\"understands\\" which type we are working with.","TypeScript can narrow types automatically, but sometimes it needs a hint from the developer.\\n\\nDefinition\\nA type predicate is a function with a return type of the form `value is Type`, which tells TypeScript that a value belongs to a particular type.\\n\\nAn example of a predicate\\nConsider a check for a custom type:\\n\\nHow it works\\nAfter such a function is used:\\n- TypeScript trusts the result of the check\\n- the variable\'s type is narrowed inside the block\\n\\nWhy this is needed\\nType predicates are useful when you:\\n- work with `unknown`\\n- use union types\\n- write reusable checks\\n- validate data from an API\\n\\nThe difference from ordinary checks\\nAn ordinary function:\\n- returns a `boolean`\\n- does not affect the type\\n\\nA type predicate:\\n- returns a `boolean`\\n- affects the type system\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nType predicates let you control type narrowing manually and make code safer and more expressive. They are a powerful TypeScript tool for complex checks.","function isString(value: unknown): value is string {\\n  return typeof value === \'string\';\\n}\\n\\nif (isString(data)) {\\n  // data has the type string\\n  data.toUpperCase();\\n}"],[1889,"JavaScript has dynamic, weak typing. Types are determined at runtime. TypeScript adds static typing at development time. This lets you find errors before the code runs. At runtime, meanwhile, it is still ordinary JavaScript.","JavaScript and TypeScript differ not in their execution syntax but in their model of working with types.\\n\\nTyping in JavaScript\\nJavaScript:\\n- is dynamically typed\\n- the type of a value can change\\n- checks happen at runtime\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nType errors only show up when the code runs.\\n\\nWhat TypeScript adds\\nTypeScript introduces:\\n- static typing\\n- type checking at compile time\\n- type annotations\\n- type inference\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nAn important point\\nTypeScript:\\n- does not change JavaScript\'s behaviour\\n- does not exist at runtime\\n- compiles to ordinary JavaScript\\n\\nAll types are removed during the build.\\n\\nThe practical benefit\\nTypeScript:\\n- reduces the number of bugs\\n- improves autocompletion\\n- makes code easier to maintain\\n- makes refactoring safer\\n\\nConclusion\\nJavaScript has dynamic typing, while TypeScript adds static type checking at development time without affecting how the code runs.","let value = 10;\\nvalue = \\"text\\";\\n\\nlet value: number = 10;\\n// value = \\"text\\"; // an error at development time"],[1890,"TypeScript helps manage the complexity of code through strict typing. It lets you find errors at development time rather than at runtime. Types improve the readability and self-documenting quality of code. Refactoring becomes safer. This matters especially when many developers work on a project.","On large projects the main problem is not writing the code but maintaining it.\\n\\nControlling complexity\\nTypeScript helps you:\\n- describe the contracts between modules explicitly\\n- restrict the allowed states of your data\\n- reduce the number of implicit assumptions\\n\\nTypes become a form of documentation that is always up to date.\\n\\nEarly error detection\\nErrors such as:\\n- wrong arguments\\n- missing fields\\n- incorrect return values\\n\\nare caught:\\n- in the IDE\\n- during the build\\n- before the application runs\\n\\nSafe refactoring\\nWhen code changes, TypeScript:\\n- highlights every place it is used\\n- does not let you \\"break\\" a contract unnoticed\\n- makes renaming and restructuring easier\\n\\nThis is critical in a codebase with thousands of files.\\n\\nBetter teamwork\\nTypeScript:\\n- sets common rules\\n- reduces dependence on verbal agreements\\n- makes onboarding new developers easier\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript is especially useful on large projects because it reduces complexity, makes code predictable and guards against errors as the system evolves.",null],[1891,"TypeScript raises the barrier to entry and takes time to learn. It adds overhead when writing code. Sometimes types become too complex. There can also be discrepancies between the types and the real runtime behaviour. On small projects its benefit may not be obvious.","Despite its advantages, TypeScript is not a free solution.\\n\\nIncreased complexity\\nIn practice these problems arise:\\n- complex generics\\n- overloaded types\\n- compiler errors that are hard to read\\n\\nThis can slow development down, especially for newcomers.\\n\\nDevelopment overhead\\nTypeScript requires you to:\\n- write types\\n- keep them up to date\\n- configure the setup (`tsconfig`)\\n\\nIn simple scenarios this looks excessive.\\n\\nA false sense of security\\nIt is important to understand:\\n- TypeScript does not check data at runtime\\n- types may not match the real data\\n- an API may return an unexpected result\\n\\nWithout runtime validation, errors are still possible.\\n\\nInfrastructure costs\\nTypeScript:\\n- increases build time\\n- complicates tooling setup\\n- adds a compilation step\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript has real drawbacks: complexity, overhead and the absence of runtime checks. Its value is not always realized and depends on the scale of the project.",null],[1892,"No, TypeScript does not have its own runtime. It is not executed directly. TypeScript is compiled to JavaScript, which is then executed in the browser or in Node.js. At runtime TypeScript disappears completely. All types are removed during compilation.","TypeScript is often thought of as a separate language, but from the point of view of execution that is not the case.\\n\\nHow TypeScript works\\nThe process looks like this:\\n- The developer writes code in TypeScript\\n- The TypeScript compiler checks the types\\n- The code is compiled to JavaScript\\n- Ordinary JavaScript is executed\\n\\nWhat happens to the types\\nTypes:\\n- exist only at development time\\n- are used for checking and autocompletion\\n- are removed completely during compilation\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nAfter compilation:\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nIt follows that:\\n- TypeScript cannot catch runtime errors\\n- types cannot be checked while the code runs\\n- validating data requires additional tools\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript has no runtime of its own and does not operate at runtime. It is a layer on top of JavaScript and exists only at development time.","function sum(a: number, b: number): number {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nfunction sum(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}"],[1893,"`any` switches off type checking for a value. TypeScript stops analysing which operations are allowed. This makes the code behave like ordinary JavaScript. Errors are no longer caught at development time. That is why people try to avoid `any` and use it only as a last resort.","The `any` type is a deliberate \\"emergency exit\\" from TypeScript\'s type system.\\n\\nDefinition\\nany is a type with which TypeScript permits any operation on a value without any checks at all.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nTypeScript will not report errors even when the code is incorrect.\\n\\nWhy `any` is dangerous\\nUsing `any`:\\n- switches off type checking\\n- breaks autocompletion\\n- makes refactoring unsafe\\n- hides real errors\\n\\nIn effect, a piece of code with `any` drops out of the type system.\\n\\nWhen `any` is used after all\\n`any` may be justified:\\n- when migrating from JavaScript\\n- when working with third-party libraries that have no types\\n- in prototypes and experiments\\n\\nEven in these cases people try to isolate it.\\n\\nAlternatives\\nInstead of `any` people more often use:\\n- `unknown`\\n- strict union types\\n- generics\\n\\nConclusion\\n`any` kills TypeScript\'s advantages, so it is avoided. The less `any` there is in the code, the more reliable and predictable the type system becomes.","let value: any = 10;\\nvalue.foo();\\nvalue();\\nvalue = \\"text\\";"],[1894,"Interfaces are convenient for describing the shape of objects and they scale well. They support extension and declaration merging. This makes them handy for public contracts and libraries. In most cases interfaces read better. That is why they are often recommended as the default.","TypeScript has two main ways of describing the structure of objects — `interface` and `type`.\\n\\nDefinition\\nAn interface is a way of describing an object\'s contract, defining its shape and the fields it offers.\\n\\nThe practical advantages of interfaces\\nInterfaces:\\n- suit object structures better\\n- are easy to extend with `extends`\\n- support declaration merging\\n- are convenient for public APIs\\n\\nAn example of extension:\\n\\nDeclaration merging\\nIf an interface is declared several times:\\n- TypeScript merges the declarations\\n- this is useful for extending types that come from libraries\\n\\nWhen `type` is better\\n`type` is preferable if:\\n- you need union types\\n- you need primitives\\n- complex type composition is involved\\n\\nConclusion\\nInterfaces are recommended as the default for describing objects and contracts. They scale better and are easier to maintain.","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Admin extends User {\\n  role: string;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Window {\\n  customProp: string;\\n}"],[1895,"If you declare an interface with the same name twice, TypeScript merges them. All the fields from both declarations end up in the resulting interface. This is called declaration merging. This behaviour is specific to interfaces. It is not available for `type`.","One of the unique features of interfaces is declaration merging.\\n\\nDefinition\\nDeclaration merging is a mechanism by which several interface declarations with the same name are combined into one.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nInterface merging is applied:\\n- in types for browser APIs\\n- when extending global objects\\n- in the declarations of third-party libraries\\n\\nLimitations\\nIt is important to remember:\\n- the fields must not conflict in their types\\n- `type` does not allow this\\n- overuse hurts readability\\n\\nPossible risks\\nUnintentional merging:\\n- can complicate debugging\\n- can make types non-obvious\\n- can lead to hidden errors\\n\\nConclusion\\nWhen an interface is declared again, TypeScript merges the declarations. This is a powerful but potentially dangerous mechanism that is worth using deliberately.","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}"],[1896,"For objects with different sets of fields, union types are normally used. Each variant is described by its own type or interface. TypeScript can narrow types based on checks. This lets you work safely with different data structures. The approach makes code clear and protected from errors.","In practice you often meet entities that share some fields but differ in the details.\\n\\nThe basic approach\\nThe most common way is union types:\\n\\nHere:\\n- `User` can be of different kinds\\n- the set of fields depends on the role\\n\\nType narrowing\\nTypeScript can determine the specific variant automatically:\\n\\nThis is called type narrowing.\\n\\nShared fields\\nShared properties are often factored out:\\n\\nAlternatives\\nDepending on the task you can use:\\n- optional fields (worse for strict typing)\\n- generics\\n- mapped types\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnion types let you describe objects with different sets of fields precisely. They are the main tool for modelling complex domain entities in TypeScript.","type User = Admin | Guest;\\n\\ninterface Admin {\\n  role: \\"admin\\";\\n  permissions: string[];\\n}\\n\\ninterface Guest {\\n  role: \\"guest\\";\\n  expiresAt: Date;\\n}\\n\\nfunction processUser(user: User) {\\n  if (user.role === \\"admin\\") {\\n    user.permissions; // available\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ninterface BaseUser {\\n  id: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Admin extends BaseUser {\\n  role: \\"admin\\";\\n}\\n\\ninterface Guest extends BaseUser {\\n  role: \\"guest\\";\\n}"],[1897,"A common user type is conveniently described with a discriminated union. To do this you introduce a shared discriminator field. From it TypeScript understands which variant of the type the code is working with. This approach is safe and scales well. It is often used in real projects.","When an entity has several clearly defined variants, a discriminated union is used.\\n\\nDefinition\\nA discriminated union is a union type in which every variant has a shared field with a literal value.\\n\\nAn example of the description\\n\\nWhy this is convenient\\nThis approach:\\n- makes type narrowing easier\\n- rules out invalid fields\\n- improves readability\\n- scales well\\n\\nAn example of use:\\n\\nWhy not optional fields\\nOptional fields:\\n- complicate the checks\\n- allow invalid states\\n- make the model less strict\\n\\nConclusion\\nA discriminated union is the best way to describe a common type for the different variants of an entity. It makes the data model strict, readable and safe.","interface BaseUser {\\n  id: number;\\n  type: \\"admin\\" | \\"user\\";\\n}\\n\\ninterface AdminUser extends BaseUser {\\n  type: \\"admin\\";\\n  permissions: string[];\\n}\\n\\ninterface RegularUser extends BaseUser {\\n  type: \\"user\\";\\n  email: string;\\n}\\n\\ntype User = AdminUser | RegularUser;\\n\\nfunction getUserInfo(user: User) {\\n  if (user.type === \\"admin\\") {\\n    user.permissions;\\n  }\\n}"],[1898,"TypeScript adds static typing on top of JavaScript and helps you find errors earlier. It improves the readability and maintainability of code, especially in large projects. JavaScript is simpler to start with but scales worse. Most production projects choose TypeScript. JavaScript remains appropriate for simple tasks.","Choosing between JavaScript and TypeScript is a choice between speed of starting and durability of the project.\\n\\nWhat JavaScript gives you\\nJavaScript:\\n- is simple to use\\n- requires no build step\\n- suits small scripts and prototypes\\n\\nThe downsides:\\n- errors are only found at runtime\\n- a large codebase is harder to maintain\\n\\nWhat TypeScript adds\\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that:\\n- introduces types\\n- checks code at development time\\n- improves autocompletion and navigation\\n\\nWhy TypeScript wins more often\\n\\n- fewer runtime errors\\n- code is easier to read and maintain\\n- it is easier to work as a team\\n- refactoring is safer\\n\\nWhen JavaScript is still appropriate\\n\\n- small projects\\n- quick prototypes\\n- scripts without long-term maintenance\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript is almost always justified in long-lived projects. JavaScript is good for quick, simple solutions.","function sum(a: number, b: number): number {\\n  return a + b\\n}"],[1899,"TypeScript catches type errors at compile time. It prevents the incorrect use of variables, functions and objects. It also helps avoid `undefined` and `null` errors. Many runtime errors turn into compile-time ones. This reduces the number of bugs in production.","TypeScript is valuable because it detects errors before the code runs.\\n\\nThe main kinds of errors\\nTypeScript helps uncover several classes of problem.\\n- Type errors\\n- passing a value of the wrong type\\n- an incorrect argument type\\n- a mismatched return value\\n\\n- Errors in object structure\\n- missing required fields\\n- typos in property names\\n- the wrong shape of data\\n\\n- Problems with `null` and `undefined`\\n- accessing fields without a check\\n- forgotten optional fields\\n\\n- API contract errors\\n- a mismatch with the server\'s response\\n- working with a DTO incorrectly\\n\\nWhat TypeScript does not catch\\n\\n- logical errors\\n- business-logic problems\\n- errors that depend on data at runtime\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript does not replace tests, but it significantly reduces one class of technical errors. It is especially useful in large, actively evolving codebases.","type User = { id: number; name: string }\\n\\nconst user: User = { id: 1 } // error"],[1900,"TypeScript makes code clearer through explicit types and contracts. From the types you can see at once what data is expected and what a function returns. This reduces the need to read the implementation in order to understand the behaviour. The code becomes self-documenting. Maintenance and refactoring get easier.","The maintainability of code depends directly on how easy it is to read and change.\\n\\nHow TypeScript improves readability\\nTypeScript adds explicit expectations to the code:\\n- argument types\\n- return value types\\n- the structure of objects\\n\\nThis lets you understand code without digging into the implementation.\\n\\nEven without the body of a function it is clear:\\n- what is required as input\\n- what will come out\\n\\nThe effect on maintenance and evolution\\nTypeScript is especially useful when:\\n- Working as a team\\n- new developers get up to speed faster\\n- there are fewer verbal agreements\\n\\n- Refactoring\\n- the IDE shows broken places immediately\\n- there are fewer hidden side effects\\n\\n- Evolving an API\\n- contract changes are visible at once\\n- it is harder to break code \\"quietly\\"\\n\\nTypeScript as documentation\\nTypes effectively act as:\\n- living documentation\\n- a checkable contract\\n- a source of autocompletion\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript makes code more explicit and predictable. This is especially valuable in large projects with a long life cycle.","function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<User> {\\n  // the implementation is hidden\\n}"]]');export{e as default};